1:
** ἄλφα**, τό,
_indeclinable_ (_plural_ τὰ ἄ.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1087a8)}}, see A a [near the start];{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 31.18, Calliasap. 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 10.453d, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 431e; ἐπίσταται δ᾽ οὐδ᾽ ἄλφα συλλαβὴν γνῶναι 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 3.22.}}
**2** T**-square,** {{Refs|11th-12th c.AD: Eustratius Philosophus “in Ethica Nicomachea commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca xx” 74.2.}}
**3** Phoenician for βοὸς κεφαλή, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**4** _metaphorically_, τὸ ἄλφα καὶ τὸ ὦ the **first** and last, {{NT|NT.Rev.1.8, +others}}

3:
**Ἀβαδδών** (Heb. אֲבַדּוֹן, **destruction**; LXX, ἀπώλεια, only in Wisdom Lit., of the place of the ruined dead: Job.26:6, 28:22, 31:12, Psa.88:12, Pro.15:11*), indecl.;
in NT, **Abaddon**, the angel of the Abyss: Rev.9:11.†
(From Abbott-Smith. LSJ has no entry)

4:
** ἀβαρής**, ές,
(βάρος) **without weight,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 277b19; ἀβαρῆ εἶναι ἀέρα καὶ πῦρ 4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.27, compare 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.143, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.9.9, etc.}}; **light,** γῆ{{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.461 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus): _metaphorically_, ἀ. χρῆμα}} a **light** matter, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Comica Adespota” 158; παρρησία.. μαλακὴ καὶ ἀ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.59c}}; of the pulse, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.651.}}
**II** **not offensive,** ὀσμαί{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 2.3}}; of persons, **not burdensome,** ἀ. ἑαυτὸν τηρεῖν, παρέχειν, {{NT|NT.2Cor.11.9, “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 5361.15 (from Berenice)}}. _adverb_ **-ρῶς** **without giving offence,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Epictetum commentaria” p.85D.}}; **without taking offence,** {{Refs|[prev. work] p.88D.}}

12:
** ἄβυσσος**, ον,
**bottomless, unfathomed,** πηγαί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.28; ἄτης ἄβυσσον πέλαγος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 470; χάσματα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1605; λίμνη 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 137}}: generally, **unfathomable, boundless,** πλοῦτος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 948; ἀργύριον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 174; φρένα Δίαν καθορᾶν, ὄψιν ἄβυσσον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1058.}}
**II** ἡ ἄ. **the great deep,** {{LXX|LXX.Gen.1.2, etc.}}: **the abyss, underworld, **{{NT|NT.Luke.8.31, NT.Rom.10.7, NT.Rev.9.1, etc.}}; **the infinite void,** {{Refs|PMag.Par. 1.1120, compare “PMag.Lond.” 121.261.}}

15:
** ἀγαθο-ποιέω**,
**do good,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Mark.3.4, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 11.70, 5th-4th c.BC: Aesopus Fabularum Scriptor 66.}}
**2** ἀ. τινά **do good to,** [NT.Luke.6.33: with _double_ _accusative {{LXX+NT|_, LXX.Num.10.32, Aristeas 242: τινί LXX.2Mac.1.2.}}]
**3** **make good,** τι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.7.22; τὰ κακά “Corpus Hermeticum” 9.4.}}
**4** Astrology texts, **make favourable,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 203.32}}: _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Julianus Laodicensis Astrologus in “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 5(1).185.}}
**4.b** **exert beneficent influence,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Paraphrasis Ptolemaei Tetrabiblou” 292.}}
**II** **do well, act rightly,** {{NT|NT.1Pet.2.15.}}

17:
** ἀγαθο-ποιός**, όν,
**beneficent,** {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Sir.42.14, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.368b, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Marcellam - Porphyrii Opuscula” 17}}; of the King of Persia, Men.Prot.{{Refs|p.16D.: with _genitive_, -ποιὲ τῆς οἰκουμένης “PMag.Lond.” 122.16.}}
**II** Astrology texts, **exerting beneficent influence,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 19, 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 4.59, “PMag.Lond.” 46.48, etc.}}
**III** **creating the Good,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 33.}}

18:
** ἀγᾰθός** [ᾰγ], ή, όν,
_Laconian dialect_ ἀγασός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1301, 5th c.AD: Cypria ἀζαθός “GDI” 57}}:— **good:**
**I** of persons,
**I.1** **well-born, gentle,** opposed to κακός, δειλός, οἷά τε τοῖς ἀγαθοῖσι παραδρώωσι χέρηες{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.324, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.275; ἀφνειός τ᾽ ἀ. τε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.664, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.276; πατρὸς δ᾽ εἴμ᾽ ἀγαθοῖο, θεὰ δέ με γείνατο μήτηρ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.109, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.611; κακὸς ἐξ ἀ. 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 190, compare 57f; πραῢς ἀστοῖς, οὐ φθονέων ἀγαθοῖς 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.71, compare 2.96, 4.285; τίς ἂν εὔπατρις ὧδε βλάστο; οὐδεὶς τῶν ἀ. κτλ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1082; οἵ τ᾽ ἀ. πρὸς τῶν ἀγενῶν κατανικῶνται [prev. author] “fragment” 84}}; τοὺς εὐγενεῖς γὰρ κἀγαθοὺς.. φιλεῖ Ἄρης ἐναίρειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 649, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 600, +others: ἀγαθοὶ καὶ ἐξ ἀγαθῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 274a}}:—in political sense, **aristocrats,** especially in the phrase καλοὶ κἀγαθοί (see. entry καλοκἀγαθός).
**I.2** **brave, valiant,** since courage was attributed to Chiefs and Nobles, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.131, +others; τῷ κ᾽ ἀγαθὸς μὲν ἔπεφν᾽, ἀγαθὸν δέ κεν ἐξενάριξεν 21.280; compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.109, etc.}}
**I.3** **good, capable,** in reference to ability, ἀ. βασιλεύς{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.179; ἰητήρ 2.732; θεράπων 16.165, 17.388; πύκτης 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 2.15; ἰητρός 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 1; προβατογνώμων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 795; ἄρχοντες 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 266}}: frequently with qualifying words, ἀ. ἐν ὑσμίνῃ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.314; βοὴν ἀ. 2.408, 563, +others; πύξ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.300; βίην 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.478; γνώμην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 687; πᾶσαν ἀρετήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 899b, cf. Alcaeus1.124e; τέχνην [prev. author] “Prt.” 323b}}; τὰ πολέμια, τὰ πολιτικά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.122, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 516b, etc.}}: more rarely with _dative_, ἀ. πολέμῳ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 4.15: with Preps., ἄνδρες ἀ. περὶ τὸ πλῆθος 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.2; εἴς τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.125a; πρός τι [prev. author] “R.” 407e}}: with _infinitive_, ἀ. μάχεσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.136; ἱππεύεσθαι 1.79}}; ἀ. ἱστάναι **good** at weighing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356b.}}
**I.4** **good,** in moral sense, first in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 438, compare 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 104, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1082, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.7.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 41d, etc.; ψυχῆς ἀγαθῆς πατρὶς ὁ ξύμπας κόσμος 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 247}}: frequently with other _adjectives_, ὁ πιστὸς κἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 541; δικαίων κἀ. [prev. work] 1050}}:—ironical, τὸν ἀ. Κρέοντα{{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 31.}}
**I.5** ὦ ἀγαθέ, **my good friend,** as a term of gentle remonstrance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 311a, etc.}}
**I.6** ἀ. δαίμων, see entry δαίμω; ἀ. τύχη, see entry τύχ; ἀ. θεός = Latin _bona dea,_ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 9, “Cic.” 19.}}
**II** of things,
**II.1** **good, serviceable,** Ἰθάκη.. ἀ. κουροτρόφος{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.27, etc.; ἀ. τοῖς τοκεῦσι, τῇ πόλει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 13.17: with _genitive_, εἴ τι οἶδα πυρετοῦ ἀ.}} **good for** it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Mem.” 3.8.3; ἑλκῶν 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.11.1.}}
**II.2** of outward circumstances, αἰδὼς οὐκ ἀ. κεχρημένῳ ἀνδρὶ παρεῖναι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.347}}; εἰπεῖν εἰς ἀγαθόν **to good purpose,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.102}}; ὁ δὲ πείσεται εἰς ἀ. περ **for his own good end,** {{Refs|11.789; οὐκ ἀγαθὸν πολυκοιρανίη 2.204:}} —ἀγαθόν [ἐστι], with _infinitive_, **it is good** to do so and so, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.282, 24.130, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.196, etc.}}
**II.3** **morally good,** πρῆξις{{NT+5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 177; ἔργα 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 112.2, cf. NT.Rom.2.7, etc.}}
**II.4** ἀγαθόν, τό, **good, blessing, benefit,** of persons or things, ὦ μέγα ἀ. σὺ τοῖς φίλοις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.20; φίλον, ὃ μέγιστον ἀ. εἶναί φασι [prev. author] “Mem.” 2.4.2, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 74, etc}} as term of endearment for a baby, **blessing!, treasure!,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 28:}} — ἀγαθόν τινα δεδρακέναι, πεποιηκέναι confer **a benefit** on.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.68, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.92}}; ἐπ᾽ ἀγαθῷ τινος for one's **good,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.27, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.4.3; ἐπ᾽ ἀ. τοῖς πολίταις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1487; οὐκ ἐπ᾽ ἀ.}}**for no good end,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.131; ἐπ᾽ οὐδενὶ ἀ. τῆς Ἑλλάδος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.35:—in _plural_, ἡ ἐπ᾽ ἀγαθοῖς γεναμένη (sic) κατασπορά “PFlor.” 21.10 (3rd c.AD):}} —τὸ ἀ. or τἀ., **the good,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 171.5, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 506b, 508e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1091a31, etc.:}} —in _plural_, ἀγαθά, τά, **goods of fortune, treasures, wealth,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.172, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.91, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.63, etc.; ἀγαθὰ πράττειν}}**fare well,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1706}}; also, **good things, dainties,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1000, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 873, etc.}}: **good qualities,** τοῖς ἀ., οἷς ἔχομεν ἐν τῇ ψυχῇ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.32, compare 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 37}}; **good points,** of a horse, εἰ τἄλλα πάντα ἀ. ἔχοι, κακόπους δ᾽ εἴη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.2.}}
**III** _comparative_ and _superlative_ are usually supplied from other stems, _i.e. _ _comparative_ ἀμείνων, ἀρείων, βελτίων, κρείσσων (κάρρων), λωΐων (λὥων), _Epic dialect_ βέλτερος, λωΐτερος, φέρτερος:—_superlative_ ἄριστος, βέλτιστος, κράτιστος, λώϊστος (λῷστος), _Epic dialect_ βέλτατος, κάρτιστος, φέρτατος, φέριστος:— later, regular _comparative_ ἀγαθώτερος{{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.Judg.11.25, 15.2, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 8 “fragment” 12, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.5.9, Diod.Rh. p.53.9H.}}: _superlative_ ἀγαθώτατος{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.85, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 5.15}} etc. (**-ότατος** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1757.26 (2nd c.AD)).}}
**IV** _adverb_ usually εὖ, which see: ἀγαθῶς{{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 9, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1388b6, LXX.1Ki.20.7. (Etym. uncertain (ὅτι ἄγει ἡμᾶς ἐπὶ τὸν ὀρθὸν βίον “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.49)}}; perhaps cognate with ἄγαμαι, hence **admirable.**)

22:
** ἄγαμ-ος**, ον,
**unmarried, single,** properly of the man, whether bachelor or widower (ἄνανδρος being used of the woman), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.40, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 9.7, etc.; ζῶ δὲ Τίμωνος βίον, ἄγαμον, ἄδουλον 5th c.BC: Phrynichus Comicus 18:—of the woman, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 143, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1502, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 205.}}_adverb_ -ως Scholia (at [prev. work])
**II** γάμος ἄ. **marriage that is no marriage, fatal marriage,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1214, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 690.}}

23:
** ἀγᾰνακτ-έω**,
properly in physical sense, **feel a violent irritation,** of the effects of cold on the body, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ὑγρῶν χρήσιος” 2, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 46.7.8}}; of wine, **ferment,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.734e}}; so _metaphorically_, ζεῖ τε καὶ ἀ., of the soul, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 251c.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **to be displeased, vexed,** μηδ᾽ ἀγανάκτει{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 287}}; especially **show outward signs of grief,** κλάων καὶ ἀ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 117d; τὰ σπλάγχν᾽ ἀγανακτεῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1006, etc.}}; ἀ. ἐνθυμούμενος.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.18:—followed by a _relative_, ἀ. ὅτι.. 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.2.1, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.3}}; ἀ. εἰ.., ἐάν.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.139, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 194a.}}
**II.2** with _dative_ _of things_, **to be vexed at** a thing, θανάτῳ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 63b, etc.; with _accusative_ _neuter_, [prev. work] 64a}}; ἀ. ταῦτα, ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Euthphr.” 4d; ἀ. ἐπί τινι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 16.49, etc.; ὑπέρ τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 283e, etc.; περί τινος [prev. author] “Ep.” 349d; διά τι [prev. author] “Phd.” 63c; πρός τι 1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 4, 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 7.66; and sometimes with _genitive_ _of things_, “Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 334.}}
**II.3** **to be vexed at** or **with** a person, τινί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.3.11; πρός τινα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 28, 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 68; κατά τινος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 18}}:—with _participle_, **to be angry at,** ἀ. ἀποθνῄσκοντας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 62e, compare 67d.}}
**III** _middle_ in _active_ sense, _aorist_ _participle_ **-ησάμενος**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Somn.” 4; probably in 4th c.AD: Palaephatus Paradoxographus 40; ἠγανάκτηνται τῷ πράγματι 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 70.}}

24:
** ἀγᾰνάκτ-ησις**, εως, ἡ,
**physical pain and irritation,** ἀ. περὶ τὰ οὖλα, of the irritation caused by teething, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 251c.}}
**II** **vexation,** ἀγανάκτησιν ἔχει{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.41, cf. NT.2Cor.7.11, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.4.19}}:—of God, **wrath,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Marcellam - Porphyrii Opuscula” 7, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “contra Galilaeos” 171e.}}

25:
** ἀγαπάω**
(_Doric dialect_ ἀγαπ-έω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.1.110)}}, _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ ἀγάπησα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.214}}: _perfect_ ἠγάπηκα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.147, etc.}}
**I** **greet with affection** (compare {ἀγαπάζω}), once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, Od.[same place]:—in Trag. only **show affection for** the dead, ὅτ᾽ ἠγάπα νεκρούς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 764, compare “Hel.” 937}}:—_passive_, **to be regarded with affection,** ξένων εὐεργεσίαις ἀγαπᾶται{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(6).70}}:— generally, **love,** ὥσπερ.. οἱ ποιηταὶ τὰ αὑτῶν ποιήματα καὶ οἱ πατέρες τοὺς παῖδας ἀγαπῶσι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330c, compare “Lg.” 928a}}; ὡς λύκοι ἄρν᾽ ἀγαπῶσ᾽ poetical cited in {{Refs|“Phdr.” 241d; ἀ. τοὺς ἐπαινέτας [prev. work] 257e}}; ἐπιστήμην, τὰ χρήματα, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 62d, +others; τούτους ἀγαπᾶ καὶ περὶ αὑτὸν ἔχει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.19; ὁ μέγιστον ἀγαπῶν δι᾽ ἐλάχιστ᾽ ὀργίζεται 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 659}}; especially of children, αὐτὸν ἐτιθηνούμην ἀγαπῶσα{{Refs|[prev. author] “Sam.” 32, etc.}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 301d, etc.; ὑπὸ τῶν θεῶν ἠγαπῆσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 61.9; ὑπὸ τοῦ φθᾶ “OGI” 90.4 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC)}}; so in LXX of the love of God for man and of man for God, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Isa.41.8, LXX.Deu.11.1, +others, cf. NT.John.3.21, NT.Rom.8.28:—as distinct from φιλέω (which see)}} implying regard rather than affection, but the two are interchanged,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.9 and 12}}; φιλεῖσθαι defined as ἀγαπᾶσθαι αὐτὸν δι᾽ αὑτόν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1371a21:—seldom of sexual love, for ἐράω, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 76, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 2; ἀ. ἑταίραν 4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 22.1 (but ἀ. ἑταίρας}} **to be fond of** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.5.4}}; ἐρωτικὴν μέμψιν ἡ ἀγαπωμένη λύει uncertain in {{NT+3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 271):—of brotherly love, NT.Matt.5.43, +others}}
**I.2** **persuade, entreat,** {{LXX|LXX.2Ch.18.2.}}
**I.3** **caress, pet,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 1.}}
**II** of things, **to be fond of, prize, desire,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 215a, 215b, etc.; τὰ χρήματα “R.” 330c; μᾶλλον τὸ σκότος ἢ τὸ φῶς NT.John.3.19}}; **prefer,** τὰ Φιλίππου δῶρα ἀντὶ τῶν κοινῇ τοῖς Ἕλλησι συμφερόντων{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.109}}:—_passive_, λιθίδια τὰ ἀγαπώμενα **highly prized, precious stones,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 110d.}}
**III** **to be well pleased, contented,** once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, οὐκ ἀγαπᾷς ὃ ἕκηλος.. μεθ᾽ ἡμῖν δαίνυσα; 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.289}}; frequently in _Attic dialect_, ἀγαπᾶν ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.36}}; more commonly, ἀ. εἰ.. **to be well content** if.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 450a, +others}}; ἐὰν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|.. [prev. work] 330b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 684, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 483c, +others}}
**III.2** with _participle_, ἀ. τιμώμενος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 475b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.8, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 169}}: with _infinitive_, οὐκ ἀ. τῶν ἴσων τυγχάνειν τοῖς ἄλλοις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.50, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 55.19, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.15.4, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.61, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 12.4, etc.}}
**III.3** with _dative_ _of things_, **to be contented with,** ἀ. τοῖς ὑπάρχουσιν ἀγαθοῖς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.21; τοῖς πεπραγμένοις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.14.}}
**III.4** with _accusative_ _of things_, **tolerate, put up with,** μηκέτι τὴν ἐλευθερίαν ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.140; τὰ παρόντα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.15; τὸ δίκαιον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 359a (_passive_),compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1398a23.}}
**III.5** rarely with _genitive_, ἵνα.. τῆς ἀξίας ἀγαπῶσιν **may be content with** the proper price, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 125.7.}}
**III.6** _absolutely_, **to be content,** ἀγαπήσαντες{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 73, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nec.” 17.}}
**III.7** with _infinitive_, **to be fond** of doing, **wont** to do, like{φιλέω}, τοὺς Λυκίους ἀγαπῶντας τὸ τρίχωμα φορεῖν {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1348a29, cf. LXX.Hos.12.7.}}

26:
** ἀγάπ-η**, ἡ,
**love,** {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.Jer.2.2, LXX.Cant.2.7, +others; ἀ. καὶ μῖσος LXX.Eccl.Ec.; uncertain reading in “PBerol.” 9859 (2nd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.52}} O; of the love of husband and wife, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Scholia 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 52.}}
**2** especially **love** of God for man and of man for God, {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Wis.3.9, Aristeas 229; φόβος καὶ ἀ. 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.283, cf. NT.Rom.5.8, NT.2Cor.5.14, NT.Luke.11.42, +others}}:—also **brotherly love, charity,** {{NT|NT.1Cor.13.1, +others}}
**II** in _plural_, **love-feast,** {{NT|NT.2Pet.2.13, NT.Jude.12.}}
**III** **alms, charity,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|PGen. 14 (4th-5th c.AD).}}
**IV** ἀγάπη θεῶν, title of Isis, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1380.109 (2nd c.AD).}}

27:
** ἀγαπ-ητός**, ή, όν,
_Doric dialect_ **ἀγαπ-ᾱτός**, ά, όν, **that wherewith one must be content** {{Refs|cf (ἀγαπάω 111)}}, hence of only children, μοῦνος ἐὼν ἀ.{{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.365; Ἑκτορίδην ἀ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.401, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.817, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 85, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 761, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.131e; Νικήρατος.. ὁ τοῦ Νικίου ἀ. παῖς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.165, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1262b23, “EE” 1233b2; αὕτη μονογενὴς αὐτῷ ἀγαπητή LXX.Judg.11.34, cf. LXX.Tob.3.10, NT.Mark.12.6 (but compare also 11.2), etc.}}; ἀγαπητός· μονογενής, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus:—so of things, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1365b16; δαπίδιον ἓν ἀ. 3rd c.BC(?): Hipparchus Comicus 1; προβάτιον 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 319.3.}}
**2** **to be acquiesced in** (as the least in a choice of evils), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.22, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 5.10.3:}} —hence, ἀγαπητόν [ἐστι] **one must be content,** εἰ.., ἐάν.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 328b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 8.16, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.220, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1076a15, etc.}}; with _infinitive_, {{Refs|“EN” 1171a20.}}
**II** of things, **desirable,** ἤθη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.10.5; βίος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 61e (_superlative_).}}
**II.2** of persons, **beloved,** ἀδελφὲ ἀγαπητέ{{LXX|LXX.Tob.3.10}}: in letters, as a term of address, {{NT|NT.Rom.12.19, compare “PGrenf.” 2.73, etc.}}
**III** _adverb_ **-τῶς** **gladly, contentedly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 735d, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.219, etc.}}
**III.2** **just enough to content one, barely, scarcely,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 218c; ἀ σωθῆναι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.45, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 89.2, etc.}}

30:
** ἀγγεῖον**,
_Ionic dialect_ **ἀγγήιον**, τό, **vessel** for holding liquid or dry substances (τοῦτο.. ξηροῖς καὶ ὑγροῖς.. ἐργασθέν, ἀγγεῖον ὃ δὴ μιἇ κλήσει προσφθεγγόμεθα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 287e)}}; of metal, ἀργύρεα ἀ. silver **jars** or **vases** for water, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.188; ἀργυρᾶ καὶ χαλκᾶ ἀ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.695b}}; ἐν ἀ. χαλκῷ **mortar**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lapidibus” 60}}; ξύλινα ἀ. **tubs**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.2}}; **vessels** for holding money, in a treasury, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.121.β᾽}}; for masons' use, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.4}}; ὀστράκινα ἀ. {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.193, LXX.Lam.4.2}}; **pails** or **buckets** used by firemen, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 20}}; **sacks** of leather, θύλακοι καὶ ἄλλα ἀ.{{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.23; τὰς ῥαφὰς τῶν ἀ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lysander” 16; for corn, LXX.Gen.42.25; for wine, LXX.1Ki.25.18; for bread, LXX.1Ki.9.7}}; **box** for petitions, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTaur.” I ii 6 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**2** **receptacle, reservoir**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 9.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 845e}}; **bed** of the sea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 111a.}}
**3** **coffin, sarcophagus,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 12(2).494 (from Lesbos), “BSA” 17.227 (Pamphyl.), etc.}}
**II** of the human or animal body, **vessel, cavity**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 4.37, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 521b6, “PA” 680b33}}; of the veins, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 511b17, +others; the lungs, [prev. author] “GA” 787b3}}; the female breast, {{Refs|[prev. author] “PA” 692a12}}; **afterbirth**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.57}}; of plants, **capsule**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.11.1}}:—later, **the body** itself, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 3.3, compare 2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 7.}}

31:
** ἀγγελία**,
_Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ **ἀγγελίη**, ἡ, (ἄγγελος) **message, tidings**, as well the substance as the conveyance thereof, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.17, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.30, etc.; ἀ. λέγουσα τάδε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.114}}; ἀγγελίην φάτο, ἀπόφασθε, ἀπέειπε, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.17, 9.422, 7.416; φέρειν 15.174; πέμπειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.114; ἐσπέμπειν 3.69; τὰς ἀ. ἐσφέρειν 1.114, 3.77:-ἐμὴ ἀ}}. **a report of** me, **concerning** me, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.337}}; ἀ. τινός **a message about** a person or thing, ἀγγελίην πατρὸς φέρει ἐρχομένοιο **news of** thy father's coming, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.408}}; ἀνέρος αἴθονος ἀ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 222; ἀ. τῆς Χίου ἀφικνεῖται 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.15; ἦλθε ἀ. τῶν πόλεων ὅτι ἀφεστᾶσι [prev. author] 1.61: ἀ. ἦλθον ἐκ τῶν πολεμίων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.14}}; with Verbs of motion, ἀγγελίην ἐλθεῖν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.140}}: _Epic dialect_ in _genitive_, τευ ἀγγελίης.. ἤλυθες{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.252; ἀγγελίης οἴχνεσκε 15.640}}; ἤλυθε σεῦ ἕνεκ᾽ ἀγγελίης (i.e. ἀγγελίης σοῦ ἕνεκα){{Refs 8th c.BC+|3.206; ἀγγελίης πωλεῖται 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 781:—wrongly explanation by Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus “Legal.” as a _masculine_}}_substantive_ ἀγγελίης.
**2** **announcement, proclamation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.4}}: **command, order,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Hymnus in Cererem 448, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.28, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.150, 7.263.}}
**3** Ἀ. personified as daughter of Hermes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.82.}}
**II** **messenger**, Ἶρις ἀ. variant {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 781.}}

32:
** ἄγγελος**, ὁ, ἡ,
**messenger, envoy**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.26, etc.; δι᾽ ἀγγέλων ὁμιλέειν τινί 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92.ζ,compare “SIG” 229.25 (from Erythrae):}} — proverbial, Ἀράβιος ἄ., of a loquacious person, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 32.}}
**2** generally, **one that announces** or **tells**, e.g. of birds of augury, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.292, 296}}; Μουσῶν ἄγγελος, of a poet, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 769; ἄγγελε ἔαρος.. χελιδοῖ 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 74}}; ἄ. ἄφθογγος, of a beacon, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 549}}; of the nightingale, ὄρνις.. Διὸς ἄ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 149: with _genitive_ _of things_, ἄ. κακῶν ἐμῶν [prev. author] “Ant.” 277; ἄγγελον γλῶσσαν λόγων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 203; αἴσθησις ἡμῖν ἄ. 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.3.3; _neuter_ _plural_, ἄγγελα νίκης 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 34.226.}}
**3** **angel**, {{LXX+NT+1st c.AD+|LXX.Gen.28.12, +others, NT.Matt.1.24, +others, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.604, etc.}}
**4** in later philosophical, **semi-divine being**, ἡλιακοὶ ἄ.{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 4.141b, compare 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 2.6, 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus}} **in R.**2.243 K.; ἄ. καὶ ἀρχάγγελοι{{Refs 5th c.AD+|“Theologumena Arithmeticae” 43.10, compare 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 183, +others}}: also in mystical and magical writings, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1.49.45, “PMag.Lond.” 46.121, etc.}}
**II** title of Artemis at Syracuse, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

34:
** ἀγέλ-η**, ἡ,
(ἄγω) **herd**, of horses, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.281; elsewhere in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always of oxen and kine, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.678, etc., compare βούνομος·}}—also, **any herd** or **company**, συῶν ἀ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 168; ἀ. παρθένων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 112; μαινάδων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1022; πτηνῶν ἀγέλαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 168, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 106}}; **shoal** of fish, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 3.639: _metaphorically_, πόνων ἀγέλαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1276:—also in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 451c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 570a27, etc., but rare in early Prose.}}
**II** in Crete and at Sparta, **bands** in which boys were trained, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Ephorus Historicus 64, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 16, 4th c.AD: Heraclides Historicus “περὶ πολιτειῶν - fragment” 15, “GDI” 4952 (from Dreros), etc.; νέων ἀ. “epigram Gr.” 223.8 (from Miletus); ἀϊθέων [prev. work]239 (from Smyrna).}}
**III** ={ἀστρικαὶ σφαῖραι} {{Refs|“Theologumena Arithmeticae” 43.6.}}

36:
** ἀγεν-ής**, ές,
(γενέσθαι) **unborn, uncreated**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 27c.}}
**II** **of no family, ignoble**, opposed to ἀγαθός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 84, compare “POxy.” 33 5.5 (2nd c.AD)}}; of things, οὐκ ἀγενεῖς στίχοι [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.568; compare “Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 336, 5th c.AD(?): Stephanus Byzantius see at {Ἀνακτορεία}.}}]
**III** **childless**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 2.10, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Harpocratio Grammaticus (ἄπαις codices).}}

39:
** ἅγιος** [ᾰ], α, ον,
**devoted to the gods:**
**I** in good sense, **sacred, holy:**
**I.1** of things, especially temples, ἈΦροδίτης ἱρὸν ἅ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.41}}; ἱρο᾽ν Ἡρακλέος ἅ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 44, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 116c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.19; θηρίον 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 147.7; νηὸν ἐπὶ τῷ χάσματι Ἥρης ἅ. ἐστήσατο 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Syr.D.” 13: generally, θυσίαι, ξυμβόλαια, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.63, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 729e (_superlative_)}}; μητρός.. ἐστι πατρὶς ἁγιώτερον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cri.” 51a; ὅρκος ἅ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 834b11}}; ἅ., τό, **temple**, {{LXX+NT|“OGI” 56.59 (from Canopus), LXX.Exo.26.33, +others, cf. NT.Heb.9.2; τὸ ἅ. τῶν ἁγίων}}**Holy of Holies,** {{NT|LXX [same place]; τὰ ἅ. τῶν ἁ. “3 Ki.” 8.6, etc., cf. NT.Heb.9.3.}}
**I.2** of persons, **holy, pure,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 522 (_anapaest meter_); λαὸς ἅ. Κυρίῳ LXX.Deu.7.6, +others}} οἱ ἅ. **the Saints,** {{NT|NT.1Cor.6.1, +others}} πνεῦμα ἅ. **the Holy Spirit,** {{NT|NT.Matt.3.11, +others}} _adverb_ ἁγίως καὶ σεμνῶς ἔχειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.25.}}
**II** in bad sense, **accursed, execrable,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 373, 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1356.59.—Never in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus, or Trag. (who use ἁγνός)}}; rare in _Attic dialect_ (see. above). (Possibly cognate with Sanskrit _yajati_ 'sacrifice'.)

40:
** ἅγιος** [ᾰ], α, ον,
**devoted to the gods:**
**I** in good sense, **sacred, holy:**
**I.1** of things, especially temples, ἈΦροδίτης ἱρὸν ἅ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.41}}; ἱρο᾽ν Ἡρακλέος ἅ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 44, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 116c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.19; θηρίον 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 147.7; νηὸν ἐπὶ τῷ χάσματι Ἥρης ἅ. ἐστήσατο 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Syr.D.” 13: generally, θυσίαι, ξυμβόλαια, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.63, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 729e (_superlative_)}}; μητρός.. ἐστι πατρὶς ἁγιώτερον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cri.” 51a; ὅρκος ἅ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 834b11}}; ἅ., τό, **temple**, {{LXX+NT|“OGI” 56.59 (from Canopus), LXX.Exo.26.33, +others, cf. NT.Heb.9.2; τὸ ἅ. τῶν ἁγίων}}**Holy of Holies,** {{NT|LXX [same place]; τὰ ἅ. τῶν ἁ. “3 Ki.” 8.6, etc., cf. NT.Heb.9.3.}}
**I.2** of persons, **holy, pure,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 522 (_anapaest meter_); λαὸς ἅ. Κυρίῳ LXX.Deu.7.6, +others}} οἱ ἅ. **the Saints,** {{NT|NT.1Cor.6.1, +others}} πνεῦμα ἅ. **the Holy Spirit,** {{NT|NT.Matt.3.11, +others}} _adverb_ ἁγίως καὶ σεμνῶς ἔχειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.25.}}
**II** in bad sense, **accursed, execrable,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 373, 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1356.59.—Never in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus, or Trag. (who use ἁγνός)}}; rare in _Attic dialect_ (see. above). (Possibly cognate with Sanskrit _yajati_ 'sacrifice'.)

41:
** ἁγιότης**, ητος, ἡ,
={ἁγιωσύνη}, {{LXX+NT|LXX.2Mac.15.2, NT.Heb.12.10.}}
**II** as title, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PGiss.” 55.5 (6th c.AD).}}

42:
** ἁγιωσύνη**, ἡ,
**holiness, sanctity,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.2Mac.3.12, NT.Rom.1.4, etc.}}
**II** as title, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PMeyer” 24.2 (6th c.AD).}}

43:
** ἀγκάλη** [ᾰ], ἡ,
**bent arm,** mostly in _plural_, ἐν ἀγκάλαις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 723, “Supp.” 481, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 351, +others; proverbial, ἐν ταῖς ἀ. περιΦέρειν τινά 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.50}}; without ἐν, ἀγκάλαις ἔχειν, περιΦέρειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 289, “Or.” 464; ἐπ᾽ ἀγκάλαις λαβεῖν [prev. author] “Ion” 761, compare “IT” 1250; ἐς ἀ. “Ion” 1598}}; πρὸς ἀγκάλαις πεσεῖν{{Refs|[prev. work] 962; ὑπ᾽ ἀγκάλαις σταθείς [prev. author] “Andr.” 747}}: rarely in _singular_, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 19 (assuming variant); Φέρειν ἐν τῇ ἀ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.61, cf. X.[same place], 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 7.4.}}
**2** **bend of knee,** [{{Refs 5th c.AD+|{5th c.AD}: Caelius Aurelianus Medicus “TP” 5.1.2.}}]
**II** _metaphorically_, **anything closely enfolding,** κυμάτων ἐν ἀγκάλαις{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 23; πετραία ἀ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1019; πόντιαι ἀ. [prev. author] “Ch.” 587, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1378; πελαγίοις ἐν ἀ. 4th c.BC: Nausicrates Comicus 1.3}}; of the air, γῆν..ἔχονθ᾽ ὑγραῖς ἐν ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 941.}}
**III** **bundle, sheaf**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1180 (1st c.BC), “PLond.” 1.131r396 (1st c.AD), “POxy.” 935.19 (3rd c.AD).}}

45:
** ἄγκῡρα**, ἡ,
**anchor,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 18.9 (see. ἄγκοινα), 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 459}}; ἄ. βάλλεσθαι, καθιέναι, μεθιέναι, ἀΦιέναι to cast **anchor,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).13, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.36, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 662, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.10}}; ἄ. αἴρειν, αἴρεσθαι to weigh **anchor,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 50, 80; ἀνέλοιο “Anthologia Graeca” 10.1 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus); τὰς νέας ἔχειν ἐπ᾽ ἀγκυρέων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.12; ὁρμίζειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.59}}; ἐπ᾽ ἀγκυρέων ὁρμεῖν ride at **anchor,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.188; νηῦς μιῆς ἐπ᾽ ἀγκύρης [οὐκ ἀσφ]αλὴς ὁρμεῦσα 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.41; ἐπ᾽ ἀγκύρας ἀποσαλεύειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.22, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1071}}; prov, ἀγαθαὶ πέλοντ᾽.. δύ᾽ ἄγκυραι 'tis good to have 'two strings to your bow', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.101; ἐπὶ δυοῖν ἀγκύραιν ὁρμεῖν αὐτοὺς ἐᾶτε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.44, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 19; ἄ. δ᾽ ἥ μου τὰς τύχας ὤχει μόνη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 277}}; ἐπὶ τῆς αὐτῆς (i.e. ἀγκύρας) ὁρμεῖν τοῖς πολλοῖς, i.e. 'to be in the same boat' with the many, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.281; εἰσζ μητρζ παῖδες ἄγκυραι βίου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 685}}; οἴκων ἄ., of a son, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 80}}; ἱερὰ ἄ., last hope, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 51.}}
**II** **pruning-hook,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.2.2.}}
**III** ={αἰδοῖον}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 191.}}

47:
** ἁγν-εία**, ἡ,
**purity, chastity**, λόγων ἔργων τε{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 864 (Lyric poetry), coupled with καθαρότης 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἱερῆς νούσου” 1, cf. NT.1Tim.4.12}}; τῶνθεῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.10; ἁγνείη δ᾽ ἐστὶ φρονεῖν ὅσια “App.Anth.” 4.18.}}
**II** **strict observance of religious duties**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 909e (_plural_), etc.}}; in _plural_, **purifications, ceremonies**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.21, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus prooem._ 10, “BGU” 1198.12, etc.}}

48:
** ἁγνίζω**,
_future_ ῐῶ (ἀφ-) {{LXX|LXX.Num.8.6}}: _perfect_ ἥγνικα{{NT|NT.1Pet.1.22}}: (ἁγνός): —**wash off, cleanse away**, especially by water (τὸ πῦρ καθαίρει.. τὸ ὕδωρ ἁγνίζει{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.263e), λύμαθ᾽ ἁγνίσας ἐμά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 655; τινὰ πηγαῖς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1039.}}
**2** **cleanse, purify**, χέρας σὰς ἁγνίσας μιάσματος{{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1324, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 126.1, LXX.Exo.19.10}}:—_middle_, **purify oneself**,{{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Josh.3.5, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1105b}}:—_passive_, ἁγνίσθητι{{LXX+NT|NT.Act.21.24; ἀπὸ οἴνου LXX.Num.6.3 (_passive_).}}
**3** especially ἁ. τὸν θανόντα **purify** the dead **by fire**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 545}}:—_passive_, σώμαθ᾽ ἡγνίσθη πυρί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1211.}}
**4** **sacrifice**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 314, “IT” 705 (_passive_).}}
**5** **hallow, consecrate**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Aristonous Lyricus 1.17 (_passive_).}}
**6** **burn up, consume**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 116; ἐπαστράψας αἰθὴρ ἥγνισε.. ἱστορίαν “Anthologia Graeca” 7.49 (from Bianor).}}

50:
** ἀγνο-έω**,
_Epic dialect_ ἀγνοι-, _3rd.pers.singular_ _subjunctive_ ἀγνοιῇσι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.218}}: _imperfect_ ἠγνόουν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.21, etc.}}: _future_ ἀγνοήσω{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus “fragment” 12, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.251, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 32.10, 54.31}}: _aorist_ ἠγνόησα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 134, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ ἠγνοίησα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.807, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 551}}, _Epic dialect_ _contraction_ _3rd.pers.singular_ ἀγνώσασκε{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.95}}: _perfect_ ἠγνόηκα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 221d, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 20.4:}} —_passive_, _future_ (of _middle_ form) ἀγνοήσομαι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.249}}; ἀγνοηθήσομαι variant in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 5:}}_aorist_ ἠγνοήθην, see below: _perfect_ ἠγνόημαι[{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.171, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 797a. (This Verb implies a form ἄγνοος, ={ἀγνώς} 11)}}]:—**not to perceive** or **recognize**; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, alm}} ost always in _aorist_, ἄνδρ᾽ ἀγνοιήσασ᾽ ὑλάει{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.15, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus [same place], 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 228a}}; mostly with _negative_, οὐκ ἠγνοίησεν **he perceived** or **knew well**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.807, etc.; μηδὲν ἀγνόει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 899.}}—Mostly with _accusative_, **to be ignorant of**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.156, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 78; πάντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 216d}}; ἑαυτοὺς ἀ. **forget** their former selves, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.74}}; τὴν πόλιν ἀ. **not to discern** the temper of the city, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 19.231; τὸν ξένον 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 2.26}}; **fail to understand**, τὸ ῥῆμα{{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Mark.9.32; περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 277d: with _genitive_ _person_ and rel. clause, ἀγνοοῦντες ἀλλήλων ὅ τι λέγομεν [prev. author] “Grg.” 517c}}: dependent clauses in _participle_, τίς.. ἀ. τὸν ἐκεῖθεν πόλεμον δεῦρο ἥξοντ;{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.15: with _conjunction_, οὐδεὶς ἀ. ὅτι.. [prev. author] 21.156, etc.; ἀγνοῶν εἰ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.5.12}}:—_passive_, **not to be known, recognized**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 4a, “Hp.Ma.” 294d, etc.; ἀγνοούμενα ὅπῃ.. ἀγαθά ἐστι [prev. author] “R.” 506a}}; ἠγνοῆσθαι ξύμπασιν ὅτι.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 797a; ὑπελάμβανον ἀγνοήσεσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.249}}; καιρὸν οὐ παρεθέντα οὐδ᾽ ἀγνοηθέντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]303,compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.171}}; τὰ ἠγνοημένα **unknown parts**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 7.1.4.}}
**II** _absolutely_, **go wrong, make a false step**, first in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 46, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.44 (uncertain reading), Isocrates 8.39}}; _participle_ ἀγνοῶν **ignorantly, by mistake**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.38, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1110b27; ἀγνοήσαντες 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.5}}: in moral sense, **to be ignorant of what is right, act amiss**, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.11.5, cf. NT.Heb. 5.2}}:—_middle_, **fail to recognize**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.630.}}

51:
** ἀγνό-ημα**, τό,
**fault of ignorance, oversight**, ψυχῆς{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “Hel.” 19, ἀ. ἕτερον προσαγνοεῖν 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.4.8, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.1, 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.3.11, LXX.Tob.3.3, NT.Heb.9.7}}; in _plural_, opposed to ἁμαρτήματα, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 5.3 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II** **ignorance**, περί τινος{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 7.2.4.}}
**III** **object of** ἄγνοια, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 7.}}

52:
** ἄγνοιᾰ**, ἡ,
(see. γιγνώσκω) **want of perception, ignorance**, ἀγνοίᾳ{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1596; ἀγνοίας ὕπο “Supp.” 499}}; ἣν ὑπ᾽ ἀγνοίας ὁρᾷς whom seeing you **pretend not to know**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 419; ἀγνοίᾳ ἐξαμαρτάνειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.38, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.92, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 577, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.64, etc.}}; opposed to ἐπιστήμη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 199d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 66b26; ἄ. κενότης ἐστὶ τῆς περὶ ψυχὴν ἕξεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 585b}}; δι᾽ ἄγνοιαν πράττειν, opposed to ἀγνοῶν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1110b25: in Logic, ἡ τοῦ ἐλέγχου ἄ.}} **ignoratio elenchi, ignorance** of the conditions of a valid proof, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 168a18, +others}}
**II** **mistaken conduct, a mistake**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.133, “Ep.” 2.19, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 27.2.2. [In Poets sometimes ἀγνοίᾱ, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 350, “Ph.” 129}}; old _Attic dialect_, _accusative_ to {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus Dion. “fragment” 11, compare 2nd c.AD: Moeris Grammaticus 191}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἀγνοίη{{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.]}}

53:
** ἁγνός**, ή, όν,
(compare ἅγιος) **pure, chaste, holy**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (only in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”), etc.:}}
**I** of places and things dedicated to gods, **hallowed**, ἑορτή{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.259}}; of frankincense, ἁγνὴ ὀδμή{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 1.7; ἄλσος “hymnus ad Mercurium” 187; τέμενος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.204; ὕδωρ [prev. author] “I.” 6(5).74; πυρὸς ἁγνόταται παγαί [prev. author] “P.” 1.21; αἰθήρ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 282}}; φάος, λουτρόν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 86, “Ant.” 1201; θύματα [prev. author] “Tr.” 287, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.126, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.38}}; of food, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 6.192c (_comparative_)}}; χρηστήρια{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 243, etc.}}; ἐν ἁγνῷ on **holy**ground, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 223, but χῶρον οὐχ ἁ. πατεῖν}} a spot not **lawful** to tread on, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 37.}}
**I.2** of divine persons, **chaste, pure**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, mostly of Artemis, χρυσόθρονος Ἄ. ἁ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.123, 18.202, etc.; also ἁ. Περσεφόνεια 11.386, compare “hymnus ad Cererem” 337}}; of Demeter, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 203, 439; Χάριτες 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 65}}; ἁ. θεαί, Demeter and Persephone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 14.204, 4.31; Apollo, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.64; Zeus, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 653, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1289}}: of the attributes of gods, θεῶν σέβας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 830.}}
**II** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, of persons, **undefiled, chaste**, of maidens, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 55, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.103, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 242}}; ἁ. αὐδά, of a maiden's voice, {{Refs|“Ag.” 245}}; of Hippolytus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 102}}; with _genitive_, λέχους ἁ. δέμας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1003; γάμων ἁ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 840d, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 223}}; ἁ. ἀπ᾽ ἀνδρὸς συνουσίας Jusj. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.78.}}
**II.2** **pure from blood, guiltless**, ἁγνοὶ τοὐπὶ τήνδε τὴν κόρην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 889; ἁ. χεῖρας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1604; μητροκτόνος.. τόθ᾽ ἁ. ὤν [prev. author] “El.” 975, compare “IA” 940}}; ὅθ᾽ ἁ. ἦν when he had been **purified**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 258: with _genitive_, ἁγνὰς χεῖρας αἵματος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 316; φόνου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 759c}}; Δάματρος ἀκτᾶς δέμας ἁ. ἴσχειν **pure** from food, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 138.}}
**II.3** generally, **pure, upright**, ἀέθλων ἁ. κρίσις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.21; ψυχῆς φιλία ἁ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.15, etc.}}
**III** _adverb_ ἁγνῶς καὶ καθαρῶς{{Refs 8th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 121, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 337; ἁ. ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.8.10.}}

