56:
** ἀγνωσία**, ἡ,
**ignorance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 9, 2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus “Herc.” 1055.15; συμφορᾶς ἀ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1204; κέρδος ἐν κακοῖς ἀ. [prev. author] “fragment” 205; διὰ τὴν ἀλλήλων ἀ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.66}}; opposed to γνῶσις, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 267b: with _genitive_, θεοῦ LXX.Wis.13 1, NT.1Cor.15.34.}}
**2** **lack of acquaintance**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 42.}}
**II** **being unknown, obscurity**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 238d.}}

57:
** ἄγνωστος**, ον,
**unknown**, τινί{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.175}}; **unheard of, forgotten**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Mimn 5.7; ἄ. ἐς γῆν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 94}}; **unfamiliar**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 149a5 (_comparative_).}}
**2** **not to be known**, ἄγνωστόν τινα τεύχειν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.191; πάντεσσι [prev. work] 397}}; ἀγνωστστατοι γλῶσσαν **most unintelligible** in tongue, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.94.}}
**3** **not an object of knowledge, unknowable**, ἄλογα καὶ ἄ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 202b; ἡ ὕλη ἄ. καθ᾽ αὑτήν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1036a9}}; in _comparative_, **harder to know**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 995a2.}}_adverb_ **-τως**{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Alcibiadem” p.52C.}}
**4** as the name of a divinity at Athens, νὴ τὸν Ἄγνωστον Ps.-{{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philopatr.” 9, cf. NT.Act.17.23; in _plural_, θεῶν.. ὀνομαζομένων ἀ. 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.1.4.}}
**II** _active_, **notknowing, ignorant of**, ψευδέων[{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.67 (variant{ἄγνωτον}),compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Halc.” 3.}}]_adverb_ -τως **inconsiderately**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.29 O.}}

58:
** ἀγορά** [ᾰγ], ᾶς,
_Ionic dialect_ ἀγορή, ῆς, ἡ, (ἀγείρω):— **assembly**, especially **of the People**, opposed to the Council of Chiefs, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.93, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.69, etc.}}; τοῖσιν δ᾽ οὔτ᾽ ἀγοραὶ βουληφόροι (i.e. Κυκλώπεσσι) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.112; ὀρθῶν ἑσταότων ἀ γένετ᾽ οὐδέ τις ἔτλη ἕζεσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.246}}; ἀ. Πυλάτιδες, of the **Amphictyonic Council** at Pylae, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 638, cf. Ion Eleg. 1.3; μακάρων ἀ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).29, compare “Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 210}}; ἀγορήνδε καλέσσασθαι, κηρύσσειν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.54, 2.51}}; ἀγορὴν ποιήσασθαι, θέσθαι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.489, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.171}}; εἰς ἀ. ἰέναι, ἀγέρεσθαι,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8.12, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.245; ἀγορήνδε καθέζεσθαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.372.}}—Not common in Prose, ἀγορὰν συνάγειν, συλλέγειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.7.3; ποιῆσαι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.27; ἀγορὰς ποιεῖσθαι 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 150}}: of the assembly in Attic demes, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 44.36, “IG” 2.585, +others; ἀ. συνέδριον φυλετῶν καὶ δημοτῶν “Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 327: in late Prose, ἀ. δικῶν προθεῖναι, καταστήσασθαι}}, = Latin _conventus agere_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista}}“Bis Acc.” 4,12: **meeting for games**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.14: _metaphorically_, μυρμήκων ἀ. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Icar.” 19: proverbial, θεῶν ἀ. 'Babel', 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus, etc.}}
**II** **place of assembly**, τοὺς δ᾽ εὗρ᾽ εἰν ἀγορῇ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.382; ἵνα σφ᾽ ἀ. τε θέμις τε 11.807, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.266; _plural_, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.16; οὔτε.. εἰς ἀ. ἔρχεται οὔτε δίκας 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 268.}}
**II.2** **market-place**, perhaps not earlier than {{Refs|“Hom.epigram” 14.5 πολλὰ μὲν εἰν ἀγορῇ πωλεύμενα, πολλὰ δ᾽ ἀγυιαῖς}}; frequently in later authors, πρυμνοῖς ἀγορᾶς ἔπι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.93; θεοὶ.. ἀγορᾶς ἐπίσκοποι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 272; μέση Τραχινίων ἀ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 424; ἀγορᾷ οὐδὲ ἄστει δέχεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.44; ὀλιγάκις.. ἀγορᾶς χραίνων κύκλον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 919}}; οἱ ἐκ τῆς ἀ. **market** people, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.18; ἐξ ἀγορᾶς εἶ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 181, etc.}}; εἰς ἀ. ἐμβάλλειν to go into **the forum**, i. e. be a citizen, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 5}}; ἐν τῇ ἀ. ἐργάζεσθαι to trade in **the market**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.31}}; εἰς τὴν ἀ. χειροτονεῖν (opposed to ἐπὶ τὸν πόλεμον) 'for **the market'**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.26; the Roman “Forum”, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.48.}}
**III** **business of the** ἀγορά:
**III.1** **public speaking, gift of speaking**, mostly in _plural_, ἔσχ᾽ ἀγοράων withheld him **from speaking**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.275}}; οἱ δ᾽ ἀγορὰς ἀγόρευον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 788, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.818; ᾠδὴν ἀντ᾽ ἀγορῆς θέμενος 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 1.}}
**III.2** **market**, ἀγορὰν παρασκευάζειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.40, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.4.11; ἀ. παρέχειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.44, etc.; ἄγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.7.33, etc.}}; opposed to ἀγορᾷ χρῆσθαι to have **supplies**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 7.6.24; τῆς ἀ. εἴργεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.67, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 29}}; ἀ. ἐλευθέρα, i. e. καθαρὰ τῶν ὠνίων πάντων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1331a31, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.3; opposed to ἀ. ἀναγκαία 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1331b11}}; generally, **provisions, supplies,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PPetr. 3p.131 (3rd c.BC), “PS” 14.354 (3rd c.BC), +others}}; in _plural_, Nic.Dam.p.6.17 {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; ἀγορὰς περικόπτειν cut off **supplies**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 10.43.}}
**III.2.b** **market, sale**, ἀ. τῶν βιβλίων, τῶν παρθένων, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ind.” 19, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 4.1, compare 4th c.BC: Nicochares Comicus 7.}}
**IV** as a mark of time, ἀ. πλήθουσα **the forenoon**, when the market-place was full, ἀγορῆς πληθυούσης{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.181; πληθούσης ἀγορᾶς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.10, compare “SIG” 695.38 (5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Mae.)}}; περὶ or ἀμφὶ ἀ. πλήθουσαν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.7, 1.8.1; ἐν ἀ. πληθούσῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 469d, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.92; also ἀγορῆς πληθώρη 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.173, 7.223; poetry, ἐν ἀ. πλήθοντος ὄχλου 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.85; πρὶν ἀ. πεπληθέναι 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 29: ἀγορῆς διάλυσις}} **the time just after mid-day**, when they went home from market, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.104, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 12.1.}}
**V** **marketday**, = Latin _nundinae_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.58.}}

59:
** ἀγοράζω** [ᾰγ],
_future_ άσω {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 633, ἀγορῶ LXX.Neh.10.31}}: _aorist_ ἠγόρασα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.18, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.149, etc.}}: _perfect_ ἠγόρακα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1352b7, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.17.4}}:—_middle_, _aorist_ ἠγορασάμην{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.55:}}_perfect_ ἠγόρασμαι (see. below):—_passive_, _aorist_ ἠγοράσθην{{Refs|[prev. author] 59.46}}: _perfect_ ἠγόρασμαι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.23, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 828}}:—**frequent the** ἀγορά, αἱ γυναῖκες ἀ. καὶ καπηλεύουσι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.35, 4.164, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 196a5, “Comica Adespota” 710}}; **occupy the market-place**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.51.}}
**2** **buy in the market**, πωλεῖν ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 625; ἐπιτήδεια ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.10}}; generally, **buy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 984, etc.}}; **farm** taxes or state-contracts, ὠνὴν ἀ.{{Refs|“PRev.Laws” 41.22, +others; τὸν Σαίτην ἀγοράσας [prev. work] 60.23}}:—_middle_, **buy for oneself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.14}}:—_passive_, διάτινος ἀ.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.25}}: _perfect_ _passive_ in _middle_ sense, ἀντὶ τοῦ ἠγοράσθαι αὐτοῖς τὸν οἶνον{{Refs|[prev. author] 35.19.}}
**3** **haunt the** ἀγορά, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 34, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 103}}; οὐδ᾽ ἀγοράσει γ᾽ ἀγένειος οὐδεὶς ἐν ἀγορᾷ nor shall any boy **lounge in the** ἀγορά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1373. [-ᾱζω (i. e. -ᾴζω) in sense 1, Hdn.Gr. 2.14.]}}

60:
** ἀγοραῖος** [ᾰγ], ον,
(_feminine_ -αία epithet of Artemis and Athena, see below):— **in, of**, or **belonging to the** ἀγορά, Ζεὺς Ἀ. as **guardian of popular assemblies**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.46, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 973 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 70}}; Ἑρμῆς Ἀ. as **patron of traffic**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 297, compare “IPE” 12.128 (from Olbia), “IG” 12(8).67 (from Thasos), 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.15.1}}; Ἄρτεμις Ἀ. at Olympia, {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.15.4}}; Ἀθηνᾶ Ἀ. at Sparta, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.11.9; generally, θεοὶ ἀ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 90.}}
**2** of things, τὰ ἀ. **details of market-business**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 425c.}}
**II** **frequenting the market**, ὁ ἀ. ὄχλος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.2.23; δήμου εἶδος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1291b19, etc.; τὸ ἀ. πλῆθος.. τὸ περὶ τὰς πράσεις καὶ τὰς ὠνὰς καὶ τὰς ἐμπορίας καὶ καπηλείας διατρῖβον [prev. work] 1291a4:}} —ἀγοραῖοι (with or without ἄνθρωποι), οἱ, **those who frequented the** ἀγορά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.93, 2.141}}; opposed to ἔμποροι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 3.13}}, but = **traders** (i.e. **sutlers**), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 2.2}}:—hence, **the common sort, low fellows**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1015, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 347c, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 6.2}}; of agitators, {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Act.17.5, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 38}}: _comparative_, **the baser sort**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus Euerg. 1}}. _adverb_ -αίως, λέγειν{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 10.11.}}
**II.2** of things, **vulgar**, σκώμματα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 750; τοὺς νοῦς ἀ. ἧττον.. ποιῶ [prev. author] “fragment” 471}}; ἀ. φιλία (opposed to ἐλευθέριος) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1162b26}}; **common**, ἄρτοι{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Lynceus Comicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.109d.}}
**III** generally, **proper to the** ἀγορά, **skilled in, suited for forensic speaking**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 11, +others:}} —ἀγοραος (i.e. ἡμέρα) **court-day, assize**, τὰς ἀ. ποιεῖσθαι{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 13.4.12}}; ἄγειν τὴν ἀ. Epist. Galb. cited in {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 14.10.21, cf. NT.Act.19.38, “IGRom.” 4.790.}}_adverb_ -ως **in forensic style**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “C. Gracchus” 4, “Ant.” 24.}}
**III.2** ἀγοραῖος, ὁ, = **tabellio, notary**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 50(26).94, “Edict.Diocl.” 7.41, “Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}}; also, **pleader, advocate**, in _plural_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 6.36.}}
**III.2.b** ἀγοραῖος, ἡ, **market-day,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae ad res Romanas pertinentes 4.1381 (from Lydia). (The distinction ἀγόραιος}} **vulgar**, ἀγοραῖος **public speaker**, drawn by {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Ammonius Epigrammaticus, etc., is probably fictitious.)}}

61:
** ἄγρα**,
_Ionic dialect_ ἄγρη, ἡ, **hunting, the chase**, (never in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”), ἄγρην ἐφέπειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.330; χαίρουσι δέ τ᾽ ἀνέρες ἄγρῃ 22.306; ἐς ἄγρας ἰέναι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 885; ἄ. ἀνθρώπων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 823e; ἁλιαδᾶν ἔχων ἀΰπνους ἄγρας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 880 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** **way of catching**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.81, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.70.}}
**II** **quarry, prey**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 442; ἄγραν ὤλεσα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 148 (Lyric poetry); εὔκερως ἄ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 64, compare 407 (_plural_); Μελέαγρε, μελέαν γάρ ποτ᾽ ἀγρεύεις ἄ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 517}}; **game**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.73, etc.}}; of fish, **draught, take,** {{NT+4th c.BC+|NT.Luke.5.9: _metaphorically_, δορὸς ἄγρα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 322 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III** Ἄγρα, ἡ, title of Artemis at Athens, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 229c}}; τὰ ἐν Ἄγρας (i.e. μυστήρια) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta Gr. “fragment” 13; τὰ πρὸς Ἄγραν “IG” 2.315; μήτηρ ἐν Ἄγρας [prev. work]273:}} — also Ἄγραι, αἱ, the precinct of Artemis Agra, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.19.6, St. Byzantine, “Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 334, etc. (With ἄγρα: ἄγρια compare θήρα: θηρία.)}}

62:
** ἀγράμμ-ᾰτος**, ον,
**illiterate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.20, 4th-3rd c.BC: Damoxenus Comicus 2.12, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 236, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.154 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus), compare 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.99}}; **unable to read** or **write,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 23a}}. _adverb_ **-τως**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.195, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.9.10.}}
**II** ={ἄγραπτος},{ἔθη} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 295a.}}
**III** of animals, **unable to utter articulate sounds**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 488a33.}}
**III.2** of sounds, **inarticulate**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Int.” 16a29, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 3.107}}; **incapable of being written**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 3.3, compare 11th-12th c.AD: Eustratius Philosophus “in Analyticorum Posteriorum librum secundum commentarium - Comm. in Arist. Graeca xxi pars i” 102.19}}; ᾠδὴ ἀ. **song without words**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 2 “fragment” 47.22.}}

64:
** ἀγρ-εύω**,
_future_ εύσω {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 85}}: _aorist_ ἤγρευσα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1204:}} — _middle_, see below:—_passive_, _aorist_ ἠγρεύθην {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca”}}, see below: (ἀγρεύς):—**take by hunting** or **fishing, catch**, ἰχθῦς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.95, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 12.6; ἄγραν ἠγρευκότες 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 434}}; of war, φιλεῖ.. ἄνδρας.. ἀγρεύειν νέους{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 554:}} —also in _middle_, θύματ᾽ ἠγρεύσασθε **ye caught** or **chose your** victim, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1163}}; τί μοι ξίφος ἐκ χερὸς ἠγρεύσω**;** why **didst thou snatch.. ?** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Andr.” 842}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.3.8, compare 3rd c.BC: Sphaerus Stoicus 1.142; ἀγρευθεὶς ἤγρευσε “Anthologia Graeca” 9.94 (6th-7th c.AD: Isidorus Hispalensis Grammaticus), 12.113 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus).}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **hunt after, thirst for**, αἷμα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 138}}; σὰν (i.e. Ἀρετᾶς) ἀ. δύναμιν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 675.11; ὕπνον “Anthologia Graeca” 7.196 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus), compare 12.125 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus)}}; but ἀγρεύειν τινὰ λόγῳ **to catch** by his words, {{NT|NT.Mark.12.13.}}

65:
** ἀγρι-έλαιος**, ον,
= {ἀγριελάινος}, σκυτάλη{{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.237 (from Erycius).}}
**II** as _substantive_, ={ἀγριελαία}, {{NT+4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.18, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.2.5, NT.Rom.11.17, etc.}}

66:
** ἄγριος**, α, ον,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.119}}; also ος, ον (not in Trag. or com). {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.88, 6th c.BC: Phocylides Lyricus 3.6, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 824a, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.36}}: _comparative_ **-ώτερος**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.60}}: _superlative_ **-ώτατος**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 564a}} (ἀγρός): **living in the fields, wild, savage.**
**I** of animals, opposed to τιθασός ἥμερος, **wild**, βάλλειν ἄγρια πάντα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.52; αἶξ, σῦς, 3.24, 9.539}}; even of flies, ἄ. φῦλα, μυίας{{Refs|19.30}}; ἵπποι, ὄνοι, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.86, etc.}}; ἄ. τέρας, of a bull, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1214; ἄ. θηρία 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.7}}; of men, **living in a wild state**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.191.}}
**I.2** of trees, opposed to ἥμερος, **wild**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 46, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.21, etc.}}; μητρὸς ἀγρίας ἄπο ποτόν of the **wild** vine, {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 614, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 896a8; ἄ. ἔλαιον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1197; ὕλη [prev. author] “OT” 476, etc.; μέλι NT.Matt.3.4.}}
**I.3** of countries, **wild, uncultivated**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 113b, “Lg.” 905b.}}
**II** mostly of men, beasts, etc.:
**II.1** in moral sense, **savage, fierce**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.96, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.199, etc., compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 567; δεσπότης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 329c; ἄ. καὶ ἀπαίδευτος [prev. author] “Grg.” 510b; ἄγριε παῖ καὶ στυγνέ 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 23.19, compare 2.54}}; ἄ. κυβευτής a **passionate** gambler, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 965}}; especially of παιδερασταί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 349 (cf. Scholia (at [prev. work])), 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.52, Aen. Gaz. “Thphr.” p.14 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**II.2** of temper, **wild, fierce**, θυμός, χόλος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.629, 4.23; λέων δ᾽ ὥς, ἄγρια οἶδεν 24.41; ἄ. πτόλεμος, μῶλος, 17.737, 398}}; ἄγριος ἄτη{{Refs|19.88}}; ἄ. ὁδοί **cruel** ways or counsels, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1274; ὀργή “OT” 344 (_superlative_)}}; ἀγριώτατα ἤθεα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.106; ἔρωτες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 81a; φιλία [prev. author] “Lg.” 837b, compare “R.” 572b, etc.}}; τὸ ἄ. **savageness**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cra.” 394e}}; ἐς τὸ -ώτερον **to harsher measures**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus [prev. cited]}}
**II.3** of things, circumstances, etc., **cruel, harsh**, δεσμά{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 177}}; νὺξ -ωτέρη **wild, stormy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.13; δουλεία 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 564a}}; σύντασις ἀ. a **violent** strain, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 46d}}; ἄ. βάρος, of strong, hot wine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 351.}}
**II.3.b** ἀ. νόσος, probably, **malignant**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 173, 265; ἄ. ἕλκος 2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus 1.16.}}
**III** _adverb_ -ίως, **savagely**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 972, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 705}}; ἄγρια δερκομένω, παίσδων, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 236, 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 1.11. [ᾱ 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}; ᾱ in _trimeter_, ᾰ in Lyric poetry {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus and 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; ᾱ?~X {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}}; ῑ metri grat., where the _ultima _ is long, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.313 (nisi to be read ἀγρίοο).]}}

68:
** ἀγρός**, οῦ, ὁ,
**field**, mostly in _plural_, **fields, lands**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.832, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.757, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.149, etc.; opposed to κῆποι, Theopomp. Hist. 89}}; _singular_, **farm**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.205}}; also in _plural_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.1}}:—**tilled land**, opposed to fallow, ἀγρὸς καὶ ἀργός, Ἀθηνᾶ{{Refs|20.167 (from Erythrae).}}
**2** **country**, opposed to town, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.182, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 884, etc.; ἀγρὸν τὰν πόλιν ποιεῖς 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 169}}; ἀγρῷ **in the country**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.188}}; ἐπ᾽ ἀγροῦ in **the country**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.190, 22.47; ἐπ᾽ ἀγρὸῦ νόσφι πόληος 1.185; in _plural_, κατὰ πτόλιν ἠὲ κατ᾽ ἀγρούς 17.18; ἐν οἴκοις ἢ 'ν ἀγροῖς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 112}}; ἐπ᾽ ἀγρῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1049; ἀγροῖσι [prev. author] “El.” 313; τὸν ἐξ ἀγρῶν [prev. author] “OT” 1051; τὰ ἐξ ἀγρῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13, compare 14; κατ᾽ ἀγρούς 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 318, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 881c; οἰκεῖν ἐν ἀγρῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 387.2}}; τὰ ἐν ἀγρῷγιγνόμενα **fruits**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.9.4, compare “An.” 5.3.9:}} —proverbial, οὐδὲν ἐξ ἀγροῦ λέγεις, ἀγροῦπλέως, i.e. boorish, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}—Rare in later Greek, {{NT|NT.Mark.15.21, “PAmh.” 2.134.5, “POxy.” 967}}. [ᾰ by nature, so always in Comedy texts, except{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 579, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 116}}; ᾱγρόθεν in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus Comedy texts 19}} is _paratragoedia_] (Cf. Sanskrit _ájras_ 'plain', probably from **aj** 'drive' (compare ἄγω), i.e. **pasture.**)

69:
** ἀγρυπν-έω**,
_perfect_ ἠγρύπνηκα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 2}}:—**lie awake, pass sleepless nights**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 471, Hp.[same place], 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 695a, etc.}}; opposed to καθεύδω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.42}}; ἀγρυπνεῖν τὴν νύκτα **to pass a sleepless** night, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “HG” 7.2.19, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 113}}; οἱ -οῦντες **sufferers from insomnia**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.64.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **to be watchful**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Wis.6.15, NT.Mark.13.33, NT.Eph.6.18; ὑπὲρ τῶν ψυχῶν NT.Heb.13.17; ἐπὶ τὰ κακά LXX.Dan.9.14}}: with _infinitive_, μηθέν σε ἐνοχλήσειν{{Refs|“PGrenf.” 2.14a3.}}
**3** with _accusative_, **lie awake and think of**, τινά{{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.2966.}}

70:
** ἀγρυπν-ία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, **sleeplessness, wakefulness**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 2.3, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 43b, etc.; in _plural_, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 42; ἀγρυπνίησιν εἴχετο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.129, compare “IG” 4.952.50 (Epid.), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 27, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 460d.}}
**II** **time of watching**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 368b; οἱ τῆς ἀ. ἄρχοντες 6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 13 “Pr.”}}
**III** of poetry, **product of sleepless nights**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 29.4. [ι- in 3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 3.511.]}}

71:
** ἄγω** [ᾰ],
_imperfect_ ἦγον, _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ ἄγεσκον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.148, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.849}}: _future_ ἄξω{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.139, etc.}}: thematic _aorist_ _imperative_ ἄξετε{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.105}}_infinitive_ ἀξέμεναι, -έμεν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.50, 111}}: _aorist 2_ ἤγαγον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.291, etc., _optative_ ἀγαγοίην 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 159:}}_aorist 1_ ἦξα rare, ἦζε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Timotheus Lyricus “Persae” 165}}, _participle_ ἄξας{{Refs|Batrachomyomachia 119}}, _infinitive_ ἄξαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.46}}: _perfect_ ἦχα{{Refs 7th c.BC+|“SIG” 1 (Abu Simbel, 7th-6th c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.111.3}}, (προ-) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.18}}, (συν-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.8; ἀγήγοχα “OGI” 219.15 (Sigeum, 3rd c.BC)}}, etc., _Doric dialect_ συν-αγάγοχα{{LXX+1st c.AD+|“Test.Epict.” 3.12; ἀγήοχα LXX.Tob.12.3, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 1.30.1, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor “De Figuris” 1.11, etc.}} (also in compounds, (εἰσ-) Ps.-Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.39}}, (κατ-) Canon Laws texts{{Refs|[prev. work]73)}}; ἀγείοχα{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 5.193 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}; ἀγέωχα (δι-) {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 4897d (Philae, 1st c.BC), “PTeb.” 5.198 (2nd c.BC)}}, etc.: _pluperfect_ ἀγηόχει{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.4.17}}:—_middle_, _future_ ἄξομαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}}, Trag.: them. _aorist 1_ ἄξοντο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.545, _imperative_ ἄζεσθε [prev. work] 505: also ἀξάμην}} (ἐσ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.34}}, (προεσ-) {{Refs|1.190, 8.20:}}_aorist 2_ ἠγαγόμην{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}, _2nd pers.singular_ ἀγάγαο{{Refs|“GDI” 5088.8 (Cretan dialect)}}:—_passive_, _future_ ἀχθήσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 292a}}, (προσ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.87, etc.}}; ἄξομαι in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1632, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 458d}}, (προσ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.115, etc.}}: _aorist 1_ ἤχθην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.3.10}}, _Ionic dialect_ ἄχθην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.30}}, _participle_ ἀχθείς{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 9:}}_perfect_ ἦγμαι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|Hdt 2.158, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 13.15}}; also in _middle_ sense, see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 2.}}
**I** **lead, carry, fetch, bring**, of living creatures, φέρω being used of things, δῶκε δ᾽ ἄγειν ἑτάροισι.. γυναῖκα, καὶ τρίποδα.. φέρειν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.512}}; βοῦν δ᾽ ἀγέτην κεράων by the horns, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.439}}; ἄ. εἰς or πρὸς τόπον, _poetry_ also with _accusative_ loci, νόστοι δ᾽ ἐκ πολέμων ἀπόνους (i.e. ἄνδρας).. ἆγον οἴκους{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 863 (Lyric poetry); Ἅιδας.. ἄγει τὰν Ἀχέροντος ἀκτάν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 811 (Lyric poetry); ἄ. τινά τινι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.386; ἵππους ὑφ᾽ ἅρματ᾽ ἄ. 3.476, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 465.}}
**I.b** _participle_ ἄγων **taking**, στῆσε δ᾽ ἄγων{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.558, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.130, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1342, etc.}}
**I.2** **take with one**, ἑταίρους{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.405, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 832, etc.}}; τι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.531, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.70}}; of a wife, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 559 (Lyric poetry) (more usually _middle_, which see).}}
**I.3** **carry off** as captives or booty, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.367, 9.594, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 340, etc.; ἄχθη ἀγόμενος παρὰ βασιλέα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.30}}; ἀγόμενος, i.e. δοῦλος, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 155, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 140, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 914e}}; Δίκην ἄγειν **to lead** Justice **forcibly away**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 220; ἡ ἐπιθυμία ἄγει 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1147a34}}; of a fowler, φῦλον ὀρνίθων ἀμφιβαλὼν ἄγει{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 343: especially in phrase ἄ. καὶ φέρειν}} **harry, ravage** a country, first in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.484 οἷόν κ᾽ ἠὲ φέροιεν Ἀχαιοὶ ἤ κεν ἄγοιεν, compare 23.512f}}; frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}} and _Attic dialect_ Prose:—in _passive_, ἀγόμεθα, φερόμεθα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1310, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 241}}: more rarely reversed, φέρουσί τε καὶ ἄγουσι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.88; ἔφερε καὶ ἦγε πάντας [prev. author] 3.39: with _accusative_ loci, φέρων καὶ ἄγων τὴν Βιθυνίδα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.2; ἦγον καὶ ἔκαον τὴν 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus [prev. work]5}}; ἄ. alone, **ravage,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 9(1).333 (_Locrian dialect_, 5th c.BC):}} —but φέρειν καὶ ἄγειν sometimes means simply **bear and carry, bring together**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 279c}}; τὴν ποίησιν φέρειν τε καὶ ἄγειν, i.e. **bring** it **into** the state, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 817a, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.2.}}
**I.4** ἄ. εἰς δίκην or δικαστήριον, ἐπὶ τοὺς δικαστάς **to carry** one before a court of justice, frequently in _Attic dialect_, πρὸς τὴν δίκην ἄ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1049; ὑπ᾽ ἐπίγνωσιν ἀχθῆναι “PTeb.” 28.11 (2nd c.BC)}}; simply ἄγειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 527a, etc.; ἐπὶ θανάτῳ ἄ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.10, etc.}}:—_passive_, ἐπὶ βασιλεῖς ἀχθήσεσθε{{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Matt.10.18, compare “PTeb.” 331.16 (2nd c.AD)}}; φόνου ἄγεσθαι{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.309e.}}
**I.4.b** _passive_, **to be confiscated**, τὰ κτήνη ἀχθήσεται πρὸς τὰ ἐκφόρια (to meet the rent) {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 27.75 (2nd c.BC).}}
**I.5** of ships, **carry** as cargo, **import**, [οἶνον] νῆες ἄγουσι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.72, etc.}}; ἵνα οἱ σὺν φόρτον ἄγοιμι (i.e. σύν οἱ) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.296.}}
**I.6** **draw on, bring on**, πῆμα τόδ᾽ ἤγαγον Οὐρανίωνες{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.547; Ἰλίω φθοράν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 406 (Lyric poetry); τερμίαν ἁμέραν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1330 (Lyric poetry); ὕπνον [prev. author] “Ph.” 638; χαράν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 174; δάκρυ [prev. author] Alcaeus1081.}}
**I.7** **bear up**, φελλοὶ δ᾽ ὥς. ἄγουσι δίκτυον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 506.}}
**I.8** **carry far and wide, spread abroad**, κλέος{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.311.}}
**I.9** in Medicine texts, **remove**, φλέγμα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος ἀνθρώπου” 6, compare “Aph.” 4.2; ἕλμινθα 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.16.}}
**II** **lead towards** a point, **lead on**, τὸν δ᾽ ἄγε μοῖρα κακὴ θανάτοιο τέλοσδε{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.602; κῆρες ἄγον θανάτοιο 2.834; οἷ μ᾽ ἀτιμίας ἄγεις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1035: also with}}_infinitive_, ἄγει θανεῖν **leads** to death, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 43: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἄγομαι τάνδ᾽ ἑτοίμαν ὁδόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 877 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὁδὸς ἄγει the road **leads**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 71, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 734, “Tab.Heracl.” 1.16, etc.}}: _metaphorically_, **tend**, ἐπὶ τὸ ἄκρον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 701e.}}
**II.2** **lead, guide**, especially in war, λαόν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.79}}; ἄ. στρατιάν, ναῦς, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.12, 8.59, etc., compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.12}}; hence _absolutely_, **march**, θᾶσσον ὁ Νικίας ἦγε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.81, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.2.19, etc.}}: simply, **go**, ἄγωμεν{{NT|NT.Mark.1.38}}; of the gods, etc., **guide**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, etc.; ἐπ᾽ ἀρετήν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 672; διὰ πόνων ἄγειν τινά [prev. author] “IT” 988.}}
**II.3** **manage**, νόῳ πλοῦτον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.47; πολιτείαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.127}}; τὴν σοφίαν **conduct** philosophical inquiry, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 172b}}; of reasoning, ἀγαγεῖν τοὺς λόγους[{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 47a21; εἰς τὸ ἀδύνατον ἄ. [prev. work] 27a15 (variant{ἀπάγοντας})}}]:—_passive_, **to be led, guided**, λογισμῷ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 431c; ἡγούμενος τῶν ἡδονῶν ἀλλ᾽ οὐκ ἀγόμενος ὑπ᾽ αὐτῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.45.}}
**II.4** **refer, attribute**, τι εἰς ἐθελοκάκησιν{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 27.15.13; τι ἐς Διόνυσον 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Syr.D.” 33.}}
**II.5** **bring up, train, educate**, ἀγόμενοις ὀρθῶς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 782d; ἤχθη τὴν λεγομένην ἀγωγήν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agesilaus” 1}}; of animals, **train**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.1.3.}}
**II.6** **reduce**, ἐς βραχὺ τὴν ἀρχήν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 1; ἐς τὸ ἥμισυ [prev. author] “Mul.” 1.78}}; of propositions, εἰς ῥᾳδιξστέραν κατασκευήν{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 1076.6.}}
**III** **draw out** in length, τεῖχος ἄ. **to draw a line of** wall, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.99; μέλαθρον εἰς ὀρόφους “Anthologia Graeca” 9.649 (1st c.AD(?): Macedonius Epigrammaticus); ὄγμον ἄ. 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.2}}; ἄ. γραμμάς **to draw** lines, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 101a16}}; ἤχθωσαν κάθετοι **let** perpendiculars **be drawn, Mete.**{{Refs|373a11}}; ἄ. ἐπίπεδον **describe** a plane, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 1.7, etc.}}:—_passive_, ἦκται ἡ διῶρυξ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.158, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.100}}; κόλπου ἀγομένου τῆς γῆς, i.e. when the land forms a bight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.99.}}
**IV** **hold, celebrate**, Ἀπατούρια, ὁρτήν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.147, 183 (more usually ἀνάγειν)}}; frequently in _Attic dialect_, ἄ. ἀγῶνα{{LXX+5th c.BC+|“IG” 1.53.33; θυσίαν, θεωρίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.10; κρεουργὸν ἦμαρ εὐθύμως ἄγειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1592; γάμους 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 336, cf. LXX.Tob.11.19 (_passive_)}}; ἐκκλησίαν{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 30}}:—_passive_, ἀγοραῖοι ἄγονται{{NT|NT.Act.19.38.}}
**IV.2** **keep, observe** a date, ἄ. τὴν ἡμέραν ταύτην πάντα τὸν χρόνον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.54, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 521; κατὰ σελήνην τὰς ἡμέρας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 626}}; **reckon**, τοὺς ἐνιαυτοὺς καθ᾽ ἥλιον{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 8.6.}}
**IV.3** **keep, observe**, ὀρθὰν ἄγεις ἐφημοσύναν[{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.20; σπονδὰς ἄ. πρός τινας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.7; εἰρήνην 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 465b, etc.: with _accusative_, as periphrastic for a _neuter_ Verb, σχολὴν ἄγειν, ={σχολάζειν}, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1238, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 376d}}]; ἡσυχίαν ἄ., ={ἡσυχάξειν}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.14; ἄ. ἀπαστίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 621}}; κρύψιν ἄ., of stars between setting and rising, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Autolycus Astronomus 2.9}}; **keep up, sustain, maintain**, νεῖκος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.31}}; γέλωτ᾽ ἄγειν **to keep** laughing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 382; ἄ. κτύπον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 182 (Lyric poetry)}}; with predicate, **maintain**, ἐλευθέραν ἦγε τὴν Ἑλλάδα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.36.}}
**IV.4** of Time, **pass**, ἀπήμαντον ἄγων βίοτον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.87}}; ποίας ἡμέρας δοκεῖς μ᾽ ἄγει; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 266; ὁ βίος οὑμὸς ἑσπέραν ἄγει 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 228, compare ὥραν ἄγειν}} **to be ripe**, τῆς γαστρὸς ὥραν ἀγούσης{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 2.14}}; ὥραν ἦγε θανάτου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Chor. p.38B.; τῆς ἡλικίας ἄγον τὸ ἄνθος [prev. author] p.53 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus; τέταρτον ἔτος ἄγων καὶ τριακοστόν 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “Lib. Propr.” 1.}}
**IV.5** of beliefs, **hold**, αἵρεσιν{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 27.15.14.}}
**V** **hold account, treat**, ἄ. ἀρετὰν οὐκ αἴσχιον φυᾶς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 7(6).22}}; ἐν τιμῇ ἄγειν or ἄγεσθαι, ἐν οὐδεμιῇ μοίρῃ ἄ., περὶ πλείστου ἄ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.134, 2.172, 9.7, etc.}}; θεοὺς ἄ. **to believe in**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 924}}; διὰ τιμῆς ἄ. τινά, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Prom.Es” 4, etc.; τὸ πρᾶγμ᾽ ἄ... ὡς παρ᾽ οὐδέν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 34; τὴν Ἀφροδίτην πρόσθ᾽ ἄ. τοῦ Βακχίου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 225; τιμιώτερον ἄ. τινά 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.81; εὐεργεσίας εἰς ἀχαριστίαν καὶ προπηλακισμὸν ἄ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.316:—with Adverbs, δυσφόρως τοὔνειδος ἦγον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 784; ἐντίμως ἄ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 528c, etc.}}:—_passive_, ἠγόμην δ᾽ ἀνὴρ ἀστῶν μέγιστος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 775.}}
**VI** **draw down** in the scale, hence, **weigh**, ἄ. μνᾶν, τριακοσίους δαρεικούς, etc., **weigh** a mina, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|300 darics, etc., 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.76, 24.129, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Philippides Comicus 9.4, etc.; ἄ. πλέον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 931b15; ἄ. σταθμόν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.96b.}}
**VII** on ἄγε, ἄγετε, see.s. _voccative_
**B** _middle_ ἄγομαι, **carry away for oneself**, χρυσόν τε καὶ ἄργυρον οἴκαδ᾽ ἄγεσθαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.35}}; **take with one**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|6.58, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 808, etc.}}; of a ship's cargo, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.20}}; **take to oneself**, δῶρον{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.9, compare 11}}; **take upon oneself**, ἄγεσθαι ἐς χεῖρας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, 4.79.}}
**B.2** ἄγεσθαι γυναῖκα **take to oneself** a wife, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.211; γυναῖκα ἄ. ἐς τὰ οἰκία 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59, etc.; ἄγεσθαί τινα ἐς δῶμα 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 410}}; simply ἄ. **marry**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.47, etc.:}}_perfect_ _passive_ ἦγμαι is used in this _middle_ sense, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 14.12.1}}; of the father, **bring home** a wife **for his son**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.10, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.34}}; of a brother, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.238}}; of friends of the bridegroom and bride, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.28, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 274}}: later in _passive_ of the wife, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PGnom.” 138 (2nd c.AD).}}
**B.3** like _active_, **bring**; διὰ στόμα ἄγεσθαι μῦθον **bring** through the mouth, i.e. **utter**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.91.}}

72:
** ἀγωγή**, ἡ,
(ἄγω) **carrying away**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.85, etc.}}; **freight, carriage**, πρὸς τὰς ἀγωγὰς.. χρῆσθαι ὑποζυγίοις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 370e, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 7.5, “PLond.” 3.948.2 (3rd c.AD).}}
**b** _intransitive_, τὴν ἀ. διὰ τάχους ἐποιεῖτο pursued his **voyage**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.29 (variant)}}; **movement**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 604b}}; ἀ. ἐπί τι **tendency** towards.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.1.}}
**2** **bringing to** or **in**, ὑμῶν ἡ ἐς τοὺς ὀλίγους ἀ. your **bringing** us **before** the council, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.85.}}
**3** **forcible seizure, carrying off, abduction**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1263, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 662; ἀγωγὴν ποιήσασθαι “PTeb.” 39.22 (2nd c.BC), compare 48.22.}}
**4** ὕδατος ἀγωγαί **aqueducts,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12(5).872 (from Tenos), compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.67.}}
**5** **load,** {{Refs|Ostr. 1168}}; **weight,** {{Refs|AB 333.}}
**6** **winding up** of engine, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 57.13 (_plural_).}}
**7** **drawing** of lines, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in primum Euclidis librum commentarius” pp.284,376F.}}
**8** **evoking**, πνευμάτων{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 3.6 (_plural_).}}
**8.b** **spell for bringing** a person, usually **love-charm,** {{Refs|PMag.Par. 1.1390.}}
**II** **leading, guidance**, ἵππου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 6.4; ἡ τοῦ νόμου, τοῦ λογισμοῦ ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 645a, compare “Plt.” 274b.}}
**II.2** **leading** of an army, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 746e (_plural_)}}; ἀ. στραταρχίας **conduct** of an expedition, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 339.29; ἡ ἀ. τῶν πραγμάτων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.8.5.}}
**II.3** **direction, training**, παιδεία μέν ἐσθ᾽ ἡ παίδων ὁλκή τε καὶ ἀ. πρὸς τὸν ὑπὸ τοῦ νόμου λόγον ὀρθὸν εἰρημένον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 659d, compare 819a; ἀ. ὀρθῆς τυχεῖν πρὸς ἀρετήν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1179b31; διὰ τὸ ἦθος καὶ τὴν ἀ. [prev. author] “Pol.” 1292b14, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Cleanthes Stoicus 1.107}}: in _plural_, **systems of education**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.173}}; especially of the public education of the Spartan youth, Λακωνικὴ ἀ.{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.32.1; Ἀγησίλαος ἤχθη τὴν λεγομένην ἀγωγὴν ἐν Δακεδαίμονι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agesilaus” 1}}; ἀ. στοιχειώδης elementary **course**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Apollonius Pergaeus Geometra “Conica” 1}} Praef.:—also of plants, **culture**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.3.2}}; of diseases, **treatment**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.414, 15.436.}}
**II.4** **way** of life, **conduct**, Archyt cited in {{NT+3rd c.BC+|5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 2.31.120 (_plural_), “PTeb.” 24.57 (2nd c.BC), “OGI” 223.15 (Erythrae, 3rd c.BC), LXX.2Mac.6.8, NT.2Tim.3.10, 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 1.6.}}
**II.5** **keeping, observance**, ἡμερῶν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoxenus Musicus “Rhythmica” 2.37; μηνῶν 1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 8.48.}}
**II.6** generally, **method, construction** (of a law), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1375b12}}; **style**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Isocrate” 20, +others; ἡ ἀ. τῶν διαλέκτων 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.1.41.}}
**II.7** **method of proof**, especially of syllogistic reasoning, λόγοι τὰς ἀγωγὰς ὑγιεῖς ἔχοντες{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.84, compare 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 759.14}}; **line of argument**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.106b.}}
**II.8** **school** of philosophers, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Sto.Herc.” 339.12, “Acad.Ind.” p.68 M., 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.145, etc.}}
**II.9** Milit., **manoeuvre, movement**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 12.7 and 10}}; **order of march**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work]11.8,compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 39.1.}}
**II.10** in Law, = Latin _actio,_ {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-6th c.AD: Codex Justinianus 4.24.1, +others}}
**III** **tempo**, in music, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 400c (_plural_), 4th c.BC: Aristoxenus Musicus “Harmonica” p.34 M., 3rd c.AD(?): Aristides Quintilianus Musicus 1.19}}; **sequence**, of a melody, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoxenus Musicus “Harmonica” p.29 M.}}; musical **style**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.1.41, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1141c.}}

73:
** ἀγών** [ᾰ], ῶνος, ὁ,
_Aeolic dialect_ ἄγωνος, ου, ὁ, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 121 (also E.cited in Scholia Il. “Oxy.” 1087.60)}}; Elean _dative_ _plural_ ἀγώνοιρ{{Refs|“GDI” 1172.26}}: (ἄγω):—**gathering, assembly**, ἵζανεν εὐρὺν ἀ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.258; λῦτο δ᾽ ἀ. 24.1, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.200; νεῶν ἐν ἀγῶνι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.428, compare 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1335.57}}: especially **assembly met to see games**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23}}; Ὑπερβορέων ἀ. Pi {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“P.” 10.30; κοινοὺς ἀ. θέντες 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 845.}}
**2** **place of contest, lists, course**, βήτην ἐς μέσσον ἀ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.685, compare 531, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.260, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 312, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.114, and especially 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.50: proverbial, ἔξω ἀγῶνος}} out of the **lists** or **course**, i.e. **beside the mark**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.44, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 21: _plural_, κατ᾽ ἀγῶνας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.259.}}
**II** **assembly** of the Greeks **at the national games**, ὁ ἐν Ὀλυμπίῃ ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.127; ὁ Ὀλυμπικὸς ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 583; Ἑλλάδος πρόσχημ᾽ ἀ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 682, compare 699}}:— hence, **contest for a prize** at the games, ἀ. γυμνικός, ἱππικός, μουσικός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.91, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 658a, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1163, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.104; οἱ τῶν λαμπάδων ἀ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 57.1}}; ἀ. τῶν ἀνδρῶν **contest** in which the chorus was composed of men, opposed to to παίδων or ἀγενείων (which see), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.18, etc.}}; ἀ. στεφανηφόρος or στεφανίτης **contest** where the prize is a crown, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.102, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1357a19}}; ἀ. χάλκεος, where it is a shield of brass, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.22; ἀ. θεματικός “IG” 14.739 (from Naples)}}; ἀργυρίτης δωρίτης{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.820d:}} —hence many phrases, ἀγῶνα καταστῆσαι establish **a contest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.1; τιθέναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.8; ποιεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.104; οὐ λόγων τοὺς ἀ. προθήσοντες [prev. author] 3.67; προηγόρευέ τε ἀγῶνας καὶ ἆθλα προυτίθει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.26; προκαλούμενος ἑαυτὸν εἰς ἀ. [prev. author] “Mem.” 2.3.17}}; τοὺς ἀ. νικᾶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 3.7.1; ἐν τοῖς ἀγῶσι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.301}}; of contests in general, εἰς ἀ. λόγων ἀφικέσθαι τινί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 335a}}; πρὸς τίν᾽ ἀγῶνας τιθέμεσθ᾽ ἀρετῆ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 863 (Lyric poetry); ἀ. σοφίας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 883.}}
**III** generally, **struggle**, πολλοὺς ἀ. ἐξιών, of Hercules, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 159; ξιφηφόρος ἀ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 584; εἰς ἀ. τῷδε συμπεσὼν μάχης 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 20, etc; ὁ Φίλιππος, πρὸς ὀν ἦν ἡμῖν ὁ ἀ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.67; ποιέειν ἢ παθεῖν πρόκειται ἀ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.11; ἀληθείην ἀσκέειν ἀ. μέγιστος [prev. work] 209: _plural_, πραγμάτων ἀγῶνας κεκτημένων 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 21; ἄπορος ἀ. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.2; ὅπλων ἔκειτ᾽ ἀ. πέρι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 936; and without περί, τῶν Ἀχιλλείων ὅπλων ἀ. [prev. work] 1240}}; ψυχῆς ἀ. τὸν προκείμενον πέρι **struggle** for life and death, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 847, compare “Ph.” 1330; πολλοὺς ἀ. δραμέονται περὶ σφέων αὐτῶν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.102; λόγων γὰρ οὐ.. ἁγών, ἀλλὰ σῆς ψυχῆς πέρι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1492, compareinfr. 5.}}
**III.2** **battle, action**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.89, etc.}}
**III.3** **action at law, trial**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.21, etc., compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 677, 744; εἰς ἀγῶνα καθιστάναι ἀνθρώπους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 24c, “R.” 494e; περὶ τῆς ψυχῆς εἰς ἀγῶνα καταστῆσαί τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 8.4.}}
**III.4** **speech** delivered in court or before an assembly or ruler, πρεσβευτικοὶ ἀ.{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.32.4; τοὺς ἐπιφανεστάτους εἰρηκότος ἀ. τούς τε δικανικοὺς καὶ τοὺς δημηγορικούς 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Amm.” 1.3, compare “OGI” 567 (Attalia, 2nd c.AD)}}; ἀ. ἐσχηματισμένοι{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 8.1, +others}}
**III.4.b** Rhet., **main argument** of a speech (opposed to προοίμιον, ἐπίλογος), in _plural_, Syr.{{Refs|“in Hermog.” 2.111, 170R., compare “Proll. Hermog.” cited in Rhetores Graeci 4.12 W.}}
**III.5** _metaphorically_, οὐ λόγων ἔθ᾽ ἁγών now is not **the time for** words, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 588}}; οὐχ ἕδρας ἀ. 'tis no **time for** sitting still, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Or.” 1291}}; ἀ. πρόφασιν οὐ δέχεται **the crisis** admits no dallying, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 331, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 421d, “Lg.” 751d}}; μέγας ὁ ἀ... τὸ χρηστὸν ἢ κακὸν γενέσθαι **the issue** is great.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 608b, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 235}}; οὐ περί τινος ὁ ἀ. **the question** is not about.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.44.}}
**III.6** **mental struggle, anxiety**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.71, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.56.4, NT.Col.2.1: in _plural_, τρόμοι καὶ ἀ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 7.}}
**III.6.b** of speakers, **vehemence, power**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 15.1 compare 26.3.}}
**IV** personified, Ἀγών, **divinity of the contest**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.26.3.}}

74:
** ἀγων-ία**, ἡ,
**contest, struggle for victory**, ἀγὼν διὰ πάσης ἀγωνίης ἔχων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.91; πολεμίων ἀ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 314, compare “Tr.” 1003; especially in games, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.52, “P.” 5.113:—also in Prose, ἐν δημοτικῇ ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.15}}; ἅπασαν ἀ. ἐκτεἶναι [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator] 60.30, etc.}}
**2** **gymnastic exercise**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 11, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 94b, “Lg.” 765c, etc.}}: generally, **exercise**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Grg.” 456df, “R.” 618b.}}
**3** of the mind, **agony, anguish**, ἐν φόβῳ καὶ πολλῇ ἀ.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.33, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 534.12 (_plural_), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 869b6; ἐν τοῖς τῆς ψυχῆς φόβοις, ἐλπίσιν, ἀγωνίαις [prev. author] “Spir.” 483a5; compare 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.248, +others, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p 56 W. (_plural_), 1st c.BC: Nicolaus Damascenus Historicus “Vit. Caes.” 9.}}

75:
** ἀγων-ίζομαι**,
_future_ **-ῐοῦμαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 992}}, etc. (in _passive_ sense, see infr. B); -ίσομαι only in late writers, as {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 1.31}} **-ισθήσομαι** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.504J.}}: _aorist_ ἠγωνισάμην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 427}}, etc.: _perfect_ ἠγώνισμαι (in _active_ sense) Id {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Ion” 939, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 993, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.31}} (_passive_, see infr. B): _aorist_ ἠγωνίσθην in _passive_ sense, infr. B: _active_ form ἀγωνίσας{{Refs|“IG” 4.429 (from Sicyon):}} —
**A** **contend for a prize**, especially in the public games, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.160, +others; πρός τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 579c, +others}}; περί τινος **about** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.26; Ὀλυμπίασιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Mi.” 364a; περὶ πρωτείων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.66; ὑπὲρ τῆς ἐλευθερίας [prev. author] 18.177: frequently with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἀ. στάδιον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.22; τῶν ἀγώνων, οὓς περὶ τῆς ψυχῆς ἠγωνίζεσθε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.262}}; ἀγῶνα.. τόνδ᾽ ἠγωνίσω thou **didst provoke** this contest, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 427, compare “Ion” 939; ἠγωνίζου τι ἡμῖ; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 530a: _metaphorically_, τὰ τῆς ψυχῆς Ὀλύμπια 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Abst.” [prev. cited]}}
**2** **fight**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.76, 82, +others; περὶ τῶν ἁπάντων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.16; πρός τινα [prev. author] 1.36, compare 8.27: with _accusative_ _cognate_, μῶν τι κεδνὸν-ίζετ; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 795; [μάχην] -ίσαντο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 637.}}
**3** **contend for the prize on the stage**, of the rhapsode, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.67}}; of the playwright, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 140, 419}}; of the actor, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.246, compare 250, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1451a8}}; of the choragus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.66: with _accusative_, δράματα “IG” 12(7).226 (from Amorgos)}}: generally, **contend for victory**, καλῶς.. ἠγώνισαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 194a, compare “Mx.” 235d}}; **argue**, ὅλῳ τῷ πράγματι about the question as a whole, {{Refs|“Hp.Mi.” 369c}}; especially **argue sophistically**, opposed to διαλέγομαι, {{Refs|“Tht.” 167e.}}
**4** of public speaking, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.7.4; ἀ. πρὸς ἀπόδειξιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 133 (4th c.BC: Theodectes Tragicus cited in Rhetores Graeci 6.19 W.).}}
**II** **contend in court**, as law term, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.7: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἀ. δίκην, γραφήν}} **fight** a cause **to the last**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.20, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.100}}; ἀ. ψευδομαρτυριῶν (i.e. γραφήν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 24.131; ἀ. ἀγῶνα 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.20, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7,39}}; ἀ, φόνον **fight against** a charge of murder, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 336}}; αὐτοῖς ἀ. τοῖς πράγμασιν **grapple with** the facts of the case, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1404a5.}}
**III** generally, **struggle, exert oneself**, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.87; εὖ ἀ. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.22, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Phocion” 37.}}
**B** _passive_, **to be decided by contest, brought to issue**, mostly in _perfect_, πολλοὶ ἀγῶνες ἀγωνίδαται{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.26}}; τὰ ἠγωνισμένα **points at issue**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 465, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.145: rarely in}}_present_, ὁ ἀγωνιζόμενος νόμος the law **on trial**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.28}}; or _aorist_, δεινὸς.. κίνδυνος ὑπὲρ τῆς.. ἐλευθερίας ἠγωνίσθη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.34}}; ἠγωνίσθη λαμπρῶς (_impersonal_) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sertorius” 21:}}_future_ _middle_ in _passive_ sense, ἀγωνιεῖται καὶ κριθήσεται τὸ πρᾶγμα **shall be brought to issue** and determined, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.7.}}

77:
** ἀδάπᾰνος**, ον,
**without expense, costing nothing**, γλυκέα κἀδάπανα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 593, cf. Telesp 7.8 H., 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 10 “fragment” 12.}}_adverb_ ἀδαπάνως, τέρψαι φρένα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|E “Or.” 1176, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.133 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus (probably).}}
**II** of persons, **not spending**, ἀ. χρημάτων εἰς τὸ δέον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Virtutibus et Vitiis” 1251b7; ἀ. καταστῆσαι τὸ κοινόν “Michel” 1007.33 (from Teos), compare “Inscription Prien.” 111.133 (1st c.BC).}}

79:
** ἀδελφ-ή**, ἡ,
_feminine_ of ἀδελφός, **sister**, Trag., E {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“fragment” 866, etc.; ὁμοπατρία ἀ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Γεωργός” 12, compare “PTeb.” 320.5 (2nd c.AD)}}: _Ionic dialect_ **ἀδελφ-εή**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.56, +others}}; _Epic dialect_ **ἀδελφ-ειή**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 1.30}}; _Doric dialect_ **ἀδελφ-εά**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.4}}, and in lyr, passages of Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 160, “OC” 535.}}
**2** **kinswoman**, {{LXX|LXX.Job.42.11.}}
**3** term of endearment, {{LXX|LXX.Cant.4.9, LXX.Tob.5.21}}; applied to a wife, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“POxy.” 744.1 (1st c.BC), etc.:—as a title, Βερενίκη ἡ ἀ. καὶ γυνὴ αὐτοῦ (of a cousin) “OGI” 60.3 (3rd c.BC)}}:—**sister** (as a fellow Christian), {{NT|NT.Rom.16.1, etc.}}

80:
** ἀδελφός** [ᾰ],
(ἀ- _copulative_, δελφύς, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 510b13}}; compare ἀγάστωρ) properly, **son of the same mother**:
**I** as _substantive_, ἀδελφός, ὁ, _vocative_ ἄδελφ; _Epic dialect_, _Ionic dialect_, and Lyric poetry ἀδελφεός (_genitive_ -ειοῦ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus is for -εόο), Cretan dialect ἀδελφιός, ἀδευφιός, “Leg.Gort.” 2.21, “Mon.Ant.” 18.319}}:—**brother**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}; ἀδελφοί **brother and sister**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 536}}; so of the Ptolemies, θεοὶ ἀδελφοί{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.30, “OGI” 50.2 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; ἀπ᾽ ἀμφοτέρων ἀδελφεός{{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.97: proverbial, χαλεποὶ πόλεμοι ἀδελφῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 975: _metaphorically_, ἀ. γέγονα σειρήνων LXX.Job.30.29.}}
**I.2** **kinsman**,{{LXX|LXX.Gen.13.8, +others}}; **tribesman, Ex.**{{Refs|2.11, +others}}
**I.3** **colleague, associate,** {{Refs|PTeb. 1.12, “IG” 12 (9).906.19 (from Chalcis)}}; **member of a college**, {{Refs|[prev. work]14.956.}}
**I.4** term of address, used by kings, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 138.3 (from Philae), 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 13.2.2, etc.; generally, LXX.Jdt.7.30; especially in letters, “PPar.” 48 (2nd c.BC)}}, etc.:—as a term of affection, applicable by wife to husband, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.Tob.10.12, “PLond.” 1.42.1 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**I.5** **brother** (as a fellow Christian), {{NT|NT.Matt.12.50, NT.Act.9.30, +others}}; of other religious communities, e.g. Serapeum, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PPar.” 42.1 (2nd c.BC),compare “PTaur.” 1.1.20.}}
**I.6** _metaphorically_, of things, **fellow**, ἀνὴρ τῷ ἀ. προσκολληθήσεται, of Leviathan's scales, {{LXX|LXX.Job.41.8.}}
**II** _adjective_, ἀδελφός, ή, όν, **brotherly** or **sisterly**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 811, etc.}}; φύσιν ἀ. ἔχοντες, of Hephaistos and Athena, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 109c.}}
**II.2** generally, of anything double, **twin, in pairs**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Mem.” 2.3.19}}:—also, **akin, cognate,** μαθήματα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 1; ἀ. νόμοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 683a: mostly with _genitive_, ἀδελφὰ τῶνδε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 192; ἡ δὲ μωρία μάλιστ᾽ ἀ. τῆς πονηρίας ἔφυ [prev. author] “fragment” 925; frequently in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, “Phd.” 108b, “Cra.” 418e, +others, compare 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Epitaphius” 35: with _dative_, ἀδελφὰ τούτοισι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1262, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 210b.}}

81:
** ἀδελφότης**, ητος, ἡ,
**brotherhood**, {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.1Mac.12.10, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 2.28, 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 38.15}}: _metaphorically_, of men and animals, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 24.108.}}
**II** **the brotherhood**, {{NT|NT.1Pet.2.17, 5.9.}}
**III** as form of address, ἡ σὴ ἀ.{{Refs|“PGrenf.” 2.89, “PAmh.” 2.156; χάριν ἀπονέμομεν τῇ ἀ. τοῦ Καίσαρος Men.Prot. p.16D.}}

82:
** ἄδηλ-ος**, ον,
**unseen, invisible**, of a fish, ποιεῖν ἑαυτὸν ἄ.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 620b31}}; ἄ. χιτών, of the hyaloid membrane of the eye, [{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus]14.712}}; **unknown, obscure**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 6; τὸν ἄ. ἄνδρα.. ἰχνεύειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 475}}; ἐὰν δὲ.. ἄ. ὁ κτείνας ᾖ {{Refs|Pl “Lg.” 874a}}; of troops, ἄ. τοῖς πολεμίοις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.3.13; εἰς τὸ ἄ. ἀποκρύπτειν [prev. author] “Eq.Mag.” 5.7.}}
**II** mostly of things, ἄ. θάνατοι death **by an unknown hand**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 496}}; ἄ. ἔχθρα **secret** enmity, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.108}}; ῥεῖ πᾶν ἄδηλον melts all **to nothing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 698}}; **inscrutable**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1318.}}
**II.b** _neuter_, ἄδηλόν [ἐστι] εἰ.. **it is uncertain** whether.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 232e, +others}}; ἄ. μή.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phd.” 91d: _absolutely_, ἄ. ὄν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.2; ἐν ἀδήλῳ εἶναι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.6; ἐν ἀδηλοτέρῳ εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.5.8; ἐξ ἀδήλου ἔρχεται [σελήνη] 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 871.5}}; also ἄ. agreeing with the subject (like δίκαιός εἰμι), παῖδες ἄ. ὁποτέρων, ={ἄδηλον ὁποτέρων παῖδές εἰσιν}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.33}}; ἀδήλοις.. πῶς ἀποβήσεται, ={ἃ ἄ. ἐστι πῶς ἀ}., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1112b9, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.6.}}
**II.2** **not evident to sense**, ὄψις τῶν ἀ. τὰ φαινόμενα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 21a, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.6U.}}; opposed to φανερόν, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 6, +others}}; opposed to ἐναργές,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 14, compare 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 8.}}
**II.3** **unintelligible**, φωνή{{NT|NT.1Cor.14.8.}}
**II.4** **unproved,** {{Refs|Stoic. 2.89.}}
**III** _adverb_ -λως **secretly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.92, etc.}}: _superlative_ **-ότατα**{{Refs|[prev. author] 7.50.}}

